import{n as e,t}from"./expand-B9sP1tsw.js";var n=[[3,1,`Which of these is a living thing?`,`A tree`,[`A rock`,`A chair`,`A cloud`],`Living things grow, need food and water, and can make more of themselves. A tree does all of this.`],[3,1,`What are the three main states of matter?`,`Solid, liquid and gas`,[`Hot, warm and cold`,`Big, medium and small`,`Hard, soft and sticky`],`Matter is usually found as a solid, a liquid or a gas.`],[3,1,`How many legs does an insect have?`,`Six`,[`Four`,`Eight`,`Ten`],`All insects have six legs and three body parts: head, thorax and abdomen.`],[3,1,`Which animal is a mammal?`,`Dolphin`,[`Shark`,`Frog`,`Eagle`],`Dolphins breathe air and feed milk to their babies, so they are mammals. Sharks are fish.`],[3,1,`What do we call animals that eat only plants?`,`Herbivores`,[`Carnivores`,`Omnivores`,`Insectivores`],`Herbivores eat plants. Carnivores eat meat, and omnivores eat both.`],[3,2,`What do plants need to make their own food?`,`Sunlight, water and air`,[`Only soil`,`Sweets and milk`,`Darkness and sand`],`Plants use sunlight, water and carbon dioxide from the air to make food. This is called photosynthesis.`],[3,2,`What happens to water when it freezes?`,`It turns into ice`,[`It turns into steam`,`It disappears`,`It turns into sand`],`Water freezes into solid ice at 0 °C.`],[3,2,`Which part of a plant takes in water from the soil?`,`Roots`,[`Leaves`,`Flowers`,`Petals`],`Roots soak up water and nutrients and hold the plant in the ground.`],[3,2,`The Sun is a…`,`Star`,[`Planet`,`Moon`,`Comet`],`The Sun is a star: a giant ball of hot, glowing gas that gives us light and heat.`],[3,2,`What force pulls things toward the Earth?`,`Gravity`,[`Friction`,`Magnetism`,`Wind`],`Gravity pulls objects toward the centre of the Earth, which is why things fall down.`],[3,2,`Which sense organ do you use to smell?`,`Nose`,[`Ears`,`Tongue`,`Skin`],`Your nose detects smells in the air.`],[3,3,`Which of these materials does a magnet attract?`,`An iron nail`,[`A plastic spoon`,`A wooden stick`,`A rubber band`],`Magnets attract iron (and a few other metals), but not plastic, wood or rubber.`],[3,3,`A caterpillar changing into a butterfly is called…`,`Metamorphosis`,[`Evaporation`,`Migration`,`Hibernation`],`Metamorphosis is a big change in body form during an animal's life cycle.`],[3,3,`Which of these is a good conductor of electricity?`,`A copper wire`,[`A rubber glove`,`A wooden spoon`,`A glass jar`],`Metals such as copper let electric current flow easily. Rubber, wood and glass are insulators.`],[3,3,`Which season is usually the coldest in the northern half of the world?`,`Winter`,[`Summer`,`Spring`,`Autumn`],`In winter the northern half of Earth is tilted away from the Sun, so it gets less heat.`],[4,1,`What is the process plants use to make food from sunlight?`,`Photosynthesis`,[`Respiration`,`Evaporation`,`Digestion`],`Photosynthesis turns sunlight, water and carbon dioxide into sugar and oxygen.`],[4,1,`Which gas do we breathe in to stay alive?`,`Oxygen`,[`Carbon dioxide`,`Helium`,`Hydrogen`],`Our bodies use oxygen to release energy from food. Plants help make the oxygen we breathe.`],[4,1,`Which organ pumps blood around the body?`,`The heart`,[`The lungs`,`The stomach`,`The brain`],`The heart is a muscle that pumps blood to every part of the body.`],[4,2,`What is usually the first link in a food chain?`,`A producer, such as a plant`,[`A predator, such as a lion`,`A decomposer, such as a fungus`,`A scavenger, such as a vulture`],`Producers make their own food from sunlight. Everything else in the chain depends on them.`],[4,2,`What do we call animals that hunt other animals for food?`,`Predators`,[`Prey`,`Producers`,`Decomposers`],`Predators hunt and eat other animals, which are called their prey.`],[4,2,`Which simple machine is a ramp?`,`An inclined plane`,[`A lever`,`A pulley`,`A wheel and axle`],`A ramp is an inclined plane. It lets you raise a heavy load with less force over a longer distance.`],[4,2,`Which organ system includes the brain, spinal cord and nerves?`,`The nervous system`,[`The digestive system`,`The skeletal system`,`The respiratory system`],`The nervous system sends messages between the brain and the rest of the body.`],[4,2,`What do bees collect from flowers to make honey?`,`Nectar`,[`Rainwater`,`Seeds`,`Leaves`],`Bees drink sugary nectar from flowers and turn it into honey in the hive.`],[4,2,`Which of these is NOT a state of matter?`,`Energy`,[`Solid`,`Liquid`,`Gas`],`Solids, liquids and gases are states of matter. Energy is not matter.`],[4,3,`What causes day and night on Earth?`,`Earth spinning on its axis`,[`Earth moving around the Sun`,`The Moon blocking the Sun`,`The Sun moving around Earth`],`Earth turns once about every 24 hours, so each place faces the Sun, then faces away.`],[4,3,`Which type of rock forms when melted rock cools and hardens?`,`Igneous`,[`Sedimentary`,`Metamorphic`,`Fossil`],`Igneous rocks form from cooled magma or lava, like granite and basalt.`],[4,3,`What do we call the curved path of a planet around the Sun?`,`An orbit`,[`An axis`,`An equator`,`A crater`],`An orbit is the path an object takes around another because of gravity.`],[4,3,`Through which of these does sound travel fastest?`,`A solid, like steel`,[`Air`,`A vacuum`,`Warm steam`],`Sound travels fastest through a solid, like steel, because its particles are packed close together. It cannot travel in a vacuum.`],[4,3,`Which of these is a renewable source of energy?`,`Solar power`,[`Coal`,`Oil`,`Natural gas`],`Solar power is renewable: the Sun keeps shining every day. Coal, oil and gas took millions of years to form and will run out.`],[5,1,`Which is the largest planet in our solar system?`,`Jupiter`,[`Saturn`,`Neptune`,`Earth`],`Jupiter is more than 11 times wider than Earth.`],[5,1,`Which planet is known as the Red Planet?`,`Mars`,[`Venus`,`Mercury`,`Saturn`],`Iron oxide (rust) in Mars' soil gives it a reddish colour.`],[5,1,`How many planets are in our solar system?`,`Eight`,[`Seven`,`Nine`,`Ten`],`Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune. Pluto is now classed as a dwarf planet.`],[5,2,`Which gas do plants take in from the air for photosynthesis?`,`Carbon dioxide`,[`Oxygen`,`Nitrogen`,`Hydrogen`],`Plants take in carbon dioxide through tiny holes in their leaves and give out oxygen.`],[5,2,`A forest, a desert and a coral reef are all examples of…`,`Ecosystems`,[`Planets`,`Seasons`,`Food chains`],`An ecosystem is a community of living things together with the place they live in.`],[5,2,`Which layer of the Earth do we live on?`,`The crust`,[`The mantle`,`The outer core`,`The inner core`],`The crust is Earth's thin, rocky outer layer.`],[5,2,`What is it called when liquid water turns into water vapour?`,`Evaporation`,[`Condensation`,`Precipitation`,`Freezing`],`Heat makes liquid water change into a gas called water vapour.`],[5,2,`What kind of energy does a moving bicycle have?`,`Kinetic energy`,[`Potential energy`,`Nuclear energy`,`Sound energy`],`Kinetic energy is the energy of movement.`],[5,2,`What is a testable prediction in an experiment called?`,`A hypothesis`,[`A conclusion`,`An observation`,`A variable`],`A hypothesis is an idea you can test with an experiment.`],[5,3,`Which of these is a mixture and not a pure substance?`,`Salt water`,[`Pure gold`,`Distilled water`,`Oxygen gas`],`Salt water is salt and water mixed together. The others are single substances.`],[5,3,`Why do we see different phases of the Moon?`,`We see more or less of its sunlit half as it orbits Earth`,[`Earth's shadow covers a different part of it each night`,`Clouds in space hide different parts of it each week`,`The Moon grows and shrinks in size as it orbits Earth`],`Half of the Moon is always lit by the Sun. As it orbits Earth, we see different amounts of that lit half.`],[5,3,`What is a fossil?`,`The preserved remains or traces of a living thing from long ago`,[`A rock that forms when hot lava cools quickly on the surface`,`A crystal that grows slowly inside a cave from dripping water`,`A layer of soil that forms from rotting leaves each autumn`],`Fossils are found in rock and show us what ancient plants and animals looked like.`],[5,3,`Which unit is used to measure force?`,`Newton`,[`Joule`,`Watt`,`Kilogram`],`Force is measured in newtons (N), named after Isaac Newton.`],[5,3,`What is the boiling point of water at sea level?`,`100 °C`,[`50 °C`,`90 °C`,`212 °C`],`Water boils at 100 °C (212 °F) at sea level.`],[5,2,`Materials that do not let electricity flow easily are called…`,`Insulators`,[`Conductors`,`Magnets`,`Fuels`],`Insulators such as rubber and plastic keep electric current from flowing.`],[6,1,`What is the basic unit of life?`,`The cell`,[`The atom`,`The organ`,`The tissue`],`All living things are made of one or more cells.`],[6,1,`What is the control centre of an animal cell called?`,`The nucleus`,[`The cell wall`,`The chloroplast`,`The vacuole`],`The nucleus holds the cell's genetic material and directs what the cell does.`],[6,1,`What do we call a change in which a new substance is formed?`,`A chemical change`,[`A physical change`,`A change of state`,`Dissolving`],`Rusting and burning are chemical changes because new substances are made.`],[6,2,`Which part of a plant cell carries out photosynthesis?`,`Chloroplast`,[`Mitochondrion`,`Ribosome`,`Nucleus`],`Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll, the green pigment that captures sunlight.`],[6,2,`Which part of a cell releases energy from food?`,`Mitochondria`,[`Chloroplasts`,`The cell membrane`,`The nucleus`],`Mitochondria are often called the cell's power stations.`],[6,2,`What is the chemical formula for water?`,`H₂O`,[`CO₂`,`O₂`,`NaCl`],`Each water molecule has two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.`],[6,2,`Which force slows a sliding object when two surfaces rub together?`,`Friction`,[`Gravity`,`Magnetism`,`Buoyancy`],`Friction acts between surfaces and turns movement into heat.`],[6,2,`What do decomposers such as fungi do?`,`Break down dead matter and return nutrients to the soil`,[`Make their own food from sunlight, water and carbon dioxide`,`Hunt and eat smaller living animals for their energy`,`Store rainwater underground for plants to use in summer`],`Decomposers recycle nutrients so plants can use them again.`],[6,3,`A metal spoon gets hot in a cup of tea. This transfer of heat is called…`,`Conduction`,[`Radiation`,`Convection`,`Reflection`],`Conduction passes heat through direct contact between particles.`],[6,3,`Which gas makes up most of Earth's atmosphere?`,`Nitrogen`,[`Oxygen`,`Carbon dioxide`,`Argon`],`Air is about 78% nitrogen and 21% oxygen.`],[6,3,`Which of these is a chemical change?`,`Baking a cake`,[`Melting ice`,`Cutting paper`,`Boiling water`],`Baking creates new substances, and you can't turn the cake back into batter.`],[6,3,`Which force makes objects float in water?`,`Buoyancy (upthrust)`,[`Friction (drag)`,`Tension (pull)`,`Gravity (weight)`],`Water pushes upward on an object. If that push is big enough, the object floats.`],[6,3,`About how fast does light travel?`,`300,000 km every second`,[`300 km every second`,`3,000 km every second`,`3 million km every second`],`Light travels about 299,792 km per second: the fastest speed there is.`],[6,3,`In which layer of the atmosphere is the ozone layer found?`,`The stratosphere`,[`The troposphere`,`The mesosphere`,`The exosphere`],`The ozone layer sits in the stratosphere and absorbs harmful ultraviolet rays from the Sun.`],[6,1,`Where in the body does most nutrient absorption happen?`,`The small intestine`,[`The large intestine`,`The oesophagus`,`The mouth`],`The small intestine has a huge surface that absorbs nutrients into the blood.`],[7,1,`Which particles are found in the nucleus of an atom?`,`Protons and neutrons`,[`Electrons and protons`,`Electrons only`,`Neutrons and electrons`],`Protons and neutrons sit in the nucleus. Electrons move around it.`],[7,1,`What is the charge of an electron?`,`Negative`,[`Positive`,`Neutral`,`It changes`],`Electrons carry a negative charge, protons positive, and neutrons none.`],[7,1,`A gas turning into a liquid is called…`,`Condensation`,[`Evaporation`,`Melting`,`Freezing`],`Cooling makes water vapour condense into droplets, like dew on grass.`],[7,2,`What is the chemical symbol for gold?`,`Au`,[`Ag`,`Gd`,`Go`],`Au comes from the Latin word 'aurum'.`],[7,2,`Which of these is an element?`,`Oxygen`,[`Water`,`Salt`,`Air`],`Oxygen is an element: it is made of only one kind of atom. Water, salt and air are compounds or mixtures.`],[7,2,`What is the pH of a neutral solution such as pure water?`,`7`,[`0`,`1`,`14`],`pH 7 is neutral. Below 7 is acidic and above 7 is alkaline.`],[7,2,`Plate tectonics explains the movement of…`,`Earth's crustal plates`,[`Ocean tides`,`The Moon`,`Wind currents`],`Earth's crust is split into plates that slowly move, causing earthquakes, volcanoes and mountains.`],[7,2,`An organism that makes its own food is called…`,`An autotroph (producer)`,[`A heterotroph`,`A decomposer`,`A parasite`],`Plants are autotrophs: they make food using sunlight.`],[7,2,`Which body system helps protect you from disease?`,`The immune system`,[`The endocrine system`,`The excretory system`,`The skeletal system`],`White blood cells and other defences make up the immune system.`],[7,3,`Which molecule carries genetic information in living things?`,`DNA`,[`ATP`,`Glucose`,`Haemoglobin`],`DNA stores the instructions for building and running an organism.`],[7,3,`Which type of plate boundary produces mid-ocean ridges?`,`Divergent`,[`Convergent`,`Transform`,`Subduction`],`At divergent boundaries plates move apart and new crust forms.`],[7,3,`How do you calculate speed?`,`Distance ÷ time`,[`Time ÷ distance`,`Distance × time`,`Mass × acceleration`],`Speed is how much distance you cover in each unit of time.`],[7,3,`What happens during a lunar eclipse?`,`Earth's shadow falls on the Moon`,[`The Moon's shadow falls on Earth`,`The Sun switches off`,`Clouds cover the Moon`],`When Earth sits between the Sun and the Moon, Earth's shadow darkens the Moon.`],[7,3,`Which gas is released when zinc reacts with an acid?`,`Hydrogen`,[`Oxygen`,`Carbon dioxide`,`Nitrogen`],`Metals such as zinc react with acids to make a salt and hydrogen gas.`],[7,3,`What is the SI unit of electric current?`,`Ampere`,[`Volt`,`Ohm`,`Watt`],`Current is measured in amperes (amps). Volts measure push, ohms measure resistance.`],[8,1,`Which of Newton's laws says every action has an equal and opposite reaction?`,`The third law`,[`The first law`,`The second law`,`The law of gravitation`],`When you push on a wall, the wall pushes back on you with the same force.`],[8,1,`Which gas is mostly responsible for the greenhouse effect among these?`,`Carbon dioxide`,[`Oxygen`,`Nitrogen`,`Argon`],`Carbon dioxide traps heat in the atmosphere. More of it makes Earth warmer.`],[8,1,`Which law says an object stays at rest or keeps moving at constant speed unless a force acts on it?`,`Newton's first law`,[`Newton's second law`,`Newton's third law`,`Ohm's law`],`This is the law of inertia.`],[8,2,`Which equation links force, mass and acceleration?`,`F = m × a`,[`F = m ÷ a`,`F = a ÷ m`,`F = m + a`],`Newton's second law: the bigger the mass or acceleration, the bigger the force.`],[8,2,`Which law links voltage, current and resistance?`,`Ohm's law`,[`Boyle's law`,`Hooke's law`,`Newton's law`],`Ohm's law: V = I × R.`],[8,2,`What is the chemical formula for table salt?`,`NaCl`,[`KCl`,`NaOH`,`CaCl₂`],`Table salt is sodium chloride: one sodium atom bonded to one chlorine atom.`],[8,2,`Which type of bond forms when atoms share electrons?`,`Covalent bond`,[`Ionic bond`,`Metallic bond`,`Hydrogen bond`],`Atoms in a covalent bond share pairs of electrons.`],[8,2,`About what is the acceleration due to gravity on Earth?`,`9.8 m/s²`,[`1.6 m/s²`,`98 m/s²`,`3.7 m/s²`],`Falling objects speed up by about 9.8 metres per second every second. The Moon's is 1.6.`],[8,2,`Which type of circuit has only one path for the current to flow?`,`A series circuit`,[`A parallel circuit`,`A short circuit`,`A radio circuit`],`In a series circuit the current has one route, so one broken part stops the whole circuit.`],[8,3,`Who proposed the theory of evolution by natural selection?`,`Charles Darwin`,[`Gregor Mendel`,`Louis Pasteur`,`Isaac Newton`],`Darwin published 'On the Origin of Species' in 1859.`],[8,3,`What is a substance that speeds up a reaction without being used up called?`,`A catalyst`,[`A reactant`,`A product`,`A solvent`],`Catalysts lower the energy needed for a reaction. Enzymes are catalysts in living things.`],[8,3,`Which process makes two identical daughter cells for growth and repair?`,`Mitosis`,[`Meiosis`,`Osmosis`,`Diffusion`],`Mitosis copies a cell's chromosomes and splits it into two identical cells.`],[8,3,`Which part of the electromagnetic spectrum has the shortest wavelength?`,`Gamma rays`,[`Radio waves`,`Microwaves`,`Visible light`],`Gamma rays have the shortest wavelength and the most energy.`],[8,3,`A solid turning directly into a gas is called…`,`Sublimation`,[`Deposition`,`Condensation`,`Evaporation`],`Dry ice (solid carbon dioxide) sublimates: it skips the liquid state.`],[8,3,`Which blood vessels carry blood away from the heart?`,`Arteries`,[`Veins`,`Capillaries`,`Valves`],`Arteries carry blood away from the heart; veins bring it back.`]],r=[[[3,1,`living`,`Which part of a plant is usually green and makes food for the plant?`,`The leaf`,[`The root`,`The flower petal`,`The seed`],`Leaves hold green chlorophyll, which traps sunlight so the plant can make its food.`],[3,1,`living`,`Which baby animal hatches from an egg?`,`A chick`,[`A puppy`,`A kitten`,`A calf`],`Hens lay eggs and chicks hatch out of them. Puppies, kittens and calves are born live.`],[3,1,`living`,`What does a tadpole grow into?`,`A frog`,[`A fish`,`A snake`,`A turtle`],`A tadpole lives in water, grows legs, loses its tail and becomes a frog.`],[3,1,`living`,`Which of these animals has feathers?`,`An owl`,[`A bat`,`A frog`,`A lizard`],`An owl is a bird, and only birds have feathers. A bat can fly too, but it has fur and is a mammal.`],[3,2,`living`,`What do we call the natural home of an animal or plant?`,`Its habitat`,[`Its diet`,`Its predator`,`Its skeleton`],`A habitat gives a living thing the food, water, shelter and space it needs.`],[3,2,`living`,`Why do many trees drop their leaves before winter?`,`To save water and energy through the cold months`,[`Because birds eat the leaves every autumn`,`Because the roots stop working for ever`,`Because the Sun burns the leaves off`],`In cold or dry seasons, dropping leaves helps a tree save water and energy until spring.`],[3,2,`living`,`Which of these animals is an amphibian?`,`A frog`,[`A shark`,`A crocodile`,`A penguin`],`Amphibians such as frogs usually start life in water and later live on land as well.`],[3,2,`living`,`How are the seeds of many plants spread to new places?`,`Wind, water and animals carry them`,[`The roots push them out underground`,`The leaves fold up and throw them`,`The stem bends down and buries them`],`Dandelion seeds float on the wind, coconuts float on water, and animals carry sticky burrs or eat fruits and drop the seeds.`],[3,3,`living`,`Which of these animals is a reptile?`,`A tortoise`,[`A whale`,`A salamander`,`A bat`],`A tortoise is a reptile, with dry, scaly skin. A salamander looks like a lizard but is an amphibian.`],[3,3,`living`,`What is a seedling?`,`A young plant that has just grown from a seed`,[`A seed that has dried out and cannot grow`,`A plant that has finished flowering and died`,`A flower that has lost all of its petals`],`When a seed germinates, the tiny new plant that comes up is called a seedling.`],[3,3,`living`,`Spiders are insects.`,`False`,[`True`],`Spiders have eight legs and two body parts, so they are arachnids, not insects.`],[4,1,`living`,`What is the main job of a flower?`,`To make seeds`,[`To take in water`,`To hold the plant up`,`To store food for winter`],`Flowers attract pollinators. Once pollinated, they make seeds that can grow into new plants.`],[4,1,`living`,`Which of these animals is a carnivore?`,`A lion`,[`A cow`,`A rabbit`,`A deer`],`Carnivores eat meat. Lions hunt other animals, while cows, rabbits and deer eat plants.`],[4,2,`living`,`What is camouflage?`,`Colours or patterns that help an animal blend in`,[`A way of keeping warm through winter`,`A loud call that warns other animals`,`A nest built high up in a tree`],`Camouflage hides an animal from predators or prey, like a stick insect that looks just like a twig.`],[4,2,`living`,`Why do many birds fly to warmer places before winter?`,`To find food and milder weather`,[`To find snow to build their nests in`,`To grow a brand-new beak`,`To get away from the longer days`],`This yearly journey is called migration. Food such as insects is scarce where winters are cold.`],[4,2,`living`,`What is pollination?`,`Moving pollen from one flower to another`,[`Seeds falling to the ground`,`Roots taking in water`,`Leaves changing colour`],`Pollen carried by insects, birds or wind lets a flower make seeds.`],[4,3,`living`,`Which animal goes through complete metamorphosis: egg, larva, pupa and adult?`,`A butterfly`,[`A grasshopper`,`A frog`,`A dog`],`A butterfly passes through egg, caterpillar (larva), chrysalis (pupa) and adult. Grasshoppers have no pupa stage.`],[4,3,`living`,`What do we call animals that are active at night and sleep in the day?`,`Nocturnal`,[`Hibernating`,`Migrating`,`Domestic`],`Nocturnal animals, such as owls, bats and hedgehogs, are active after dark.`],[4,1,`living`,`Bats are birds.`,`False`,[`True`],`Bats fly, but they have fur, give birth to live young and feed them milk, so they are mammals.`],[5,1,`living`,`What are the tiny openings on a leaf that let gases in and out called?`,`Stomata`,[`Root hairs`,`Veins`,`Sepals`],`Stomata (one is a stoma) open to let carbon dioxide in and oxygen and water vapour out.`],[5,2,`living`,`Which group of living things do mushrooms belong to?`,`Fungi`,[`Plants`,`Animals`,`Bacteria`],`Mushrooms are fungi. They cannot make their own food; they absorb it from what they grow on.`],[5,2,`living`,`What is a food web?`,`Many food chains linked together`,[`A spider's web full of insects`,`A list of the foods people eat`,`A single plant and all its fruit`],`In a food web most animals eat, and are eaten by, more than one kind of living thing.`],[5,3,`living`,`What is an adaptation?`,`A feature that helps a living thing survive where it lives`,[`A sudden change in the weather of a habitat`,`A list showing who eats whom in a habitat`,`A way of measuring how big an animal grows`],`A camel's wide feet for sand and a polar bear's thick fur are adaptations.`],[5,3,`living`,`Why are bees important for many crops?`,`They carry pollen between flowers so fruits and seeds form`,[`They eat the pests that attack the roots`,`They make the soil richer with their honey`,`They keep birds away from the fields`],`Without pollinators such as bees, many fruit and vegetable plants could not make fruit.`],[5,2,`living`,`Whales breathe air through lungs.`,`True`,[`False`],`Whales are mammals. They come to the surface to breathe through a blowhole on top of the head.`],[6,1,`living`,`Into which two main groups are animals sorted by whether they have a backbone?`,`Vertebrates and invertebrates`,[`Mammals and reptiles`,`Producers and consumers`,`Herbivores and carnivores`],`Vertebrates (fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds, mammals) have backbones; invertebrates such as insects and worms do not.`],[6,2,`living`,`What is the green pigment in plants that absorbs light?`,`Chlorophyll`,[`Haemoglobin`,`Melanin`,`Keratin`],`Chlorophyll captures light energy for photosynthesis. Haemoglobin is in blood, melanin colours skin, keratin makes hair.`],[6,2,`living`,`What happens to the number of rabbits in a field if all the foxes are removed?`,`It rises at first, because fewer rabbits are eaten`,[`It falls at once, because foxes help rabbits`,`It stays exactly the same for ever`,`It drops to zero within a day`],`Removing a predator lets prey numbers grow, until food runs short.`],[6,3,`living`,`What are living things whose cells have no nucleus, such as bacteria, called?`,`Prokaryotes`,[`Eukaryotes`,`Vertebrates`,`Fungi`],`Bacteria are prokaryotes: their genetic material floats freely in the cell. Plants, animals and fungi are eukaryotes, with a nucleus.`],[6,3,`living`,`In which part of a flower does pollen form?`,`The anther`,[`The stigma`,`The ovary`,`The sepal`],`The anther, on the end of the stamen, makes pollen. The stigma is where pollen lands.`],[6,1,`living`,`Plants give off oxygen during photosynthesis.`,`True`,[`False`],`Photosynthesis uses carbon dioxide and water and releases oxygen as a by-product.`],[7,1,`living`,`What do we call an organism's complete set of genetic instructions?`,`Its genome`,[`Its habitat`,`Its niche`,`Its biome`],`A genome is all the DNA of an organism. A niche is its role in an ecosystem.`],[7,2,`living`,`Which process do plant and animal cells use to release energy from glucose?`,`Respiration`,[`Photosynthesis`,`Transpiration`,`Pollination`],`Cells break down glucose with oxygen in respiration, releasing energy, carbon dioxide and water.`],[7,2,`living`,`What is transpiration?`,`Water evaporating from a plant's leaves`,[`A plant making food from light`,`A plant leaning towards light`,`Seeds spreading on the wind`],`Transpiration pulls water up from the roots, through the stem, to the leaves.`],[7,3,`living`,`What is biodiversity?`,`The variety of living things in an area`,[`The number of rocks in a habitat`,`How fast plants grow in spring`,`The amount of rain in a forest`],`High biodiversity means many different species, which makes an ecosystem more stable.`],[7,3,`living`,`Which term describes two species that both benefit from living together?`,`Mutualism`,[`Parasitism`,`Predation`,`Competition`],`Bees and flowers are an example: the bee gets nectar, the flower gets pollinated.`],[7,2,`living`,`Viruses are made of cells.`,`False`,[`True`],`Viruses are not cells. They are tiny packages of genetic material that can only multiply inside living cells.`],[8,1,`living`,`What is the name for a section of DNA that carries the code for one feature?`,`A gene`,[`A chromosome pair`,`A cell wall`,`An enzyme`],`Genes are sections of DNA. Many genes together make up a chromosome.`],[8,2,`living`,`If a parent cell has 46 chromosomes, how many does each sex cell (egg or sperm) have?`,`23`,[`46`,`92`,`12`],`Sex cells are made by meiosis, which halves the number, so fertilisation gives 46 again.`],[8,2,`living`,`Which scientist is known for experiments on pea plants that founded genetics?`,`Gregor Mendel`,[`Louis Pasteur`,`Isaac Newton`,`Marie Curie`],`Mendel bred thousands of pea plants in the 1850s–60s and found how features are inherited.`],[8,3,`living`,`What is the main role of enzymes in living things?`,`They speed up chemical reactions`,[`They carry oxygen in the blood`,`They store genetic information`,`They make cell walls rigid`],`Enzymes are biological catalysts: digestive enzymes, for example, break food into small molecules.`],[8,3,`living`,`Why can antibiotics not cure a cold?`,`Colds are caused by viruses, and antibiotics only kill bacteria`,[`Colds are caused by fungi that antibiotics feed`,`Antibiotics only work in the summer`,`Colds are caused by a lack of vitamins only`],`Using antibiotics when they are not needed also helps bacteria become resistant.`],[8,2,`living`,`Extinction means that every member of a species has died out.`,`True`,[`False`],`Once a species is extinct, like the dodo, it can never come back naturally.`],[3,1,`body`,`Which part of the body do you use to see?`,`The eyes`,[`The ears`,`The knees`,`The tongue`],`Your eyes take in light so your brain can make pictures of the world.`],[3,1,`body`,`How many teeth does a full set of adult teeth have?`,`32`,[`20`,`12`,`50`],`Most adults have 32 permanent teeth, including four wisdom teeth. Children have 20 baby teeth.`],[3,2,`body`,`Why should you wash your hands before eating?`,`To wash away germs that can make you ill`,[`To make your hands grow stronger`,`To stop your nails from growing`,`To make the food taste sweeter`],`Soap and water remove germs that could otherwise get into your mouth with your food.`],[3,2,`body`,`Which foods help build strong muscles?`,`Protein foods, like eggs, beans and fish`,[`Sugary foods, like sweets and cake`,`Fatty foods, like butter and oil`,`Salty snacks, like crisps`],`Protein is the body's building material for muscles, skin and other tissues.`],[3,3,`body`,`What does your skeleton do?`,`It supports your body and protects organs`,[`It digests the food you eat`,`It pumps blood around you`,`It lets you breathe in air`],`Bones hold you up, protect organs such as the brain and heart, and work with muscles to move you.`],[3,3,`body`,`Which part of the body has taste buds?`,`The tongue`,[`The ears`,`The elbows`,`The eyelids`],`Taste buds on your tongue sense sweet, salty, sour, bitter and savoury (umami) flavours.`],[3,1,`body`,`You need sleep to stay healthy.`,`True`,[`False`],`During sleep the body grows, repairs itself and the brain sorts out what you learned.`],[4,1,`body`,`Which organs do you use to breathe?`,`The lungs`,[`The kidneys`,`The liver`,`The stomach`],`Your lungs take in oxygen from the air and get rid of carbon dioxide.`],[4,2,`body`,`What carries messages between your brain and the rest of your body?`,`Nerves`,[`Bones`,`Hair`,`Fingernails`],`Nerves are like wires: signals travel along them to and from the brain very quickly.`],[4,2,`body`,`Why does your body need water?`,`To work properly and to stay cool`,[`To make your bones softer`,`To stop you growing taller`,`To turn into new muscle`],`Water is in your blood and cells, carries food around, and sweat made of water cools you down.`],[4,3,`body`,`Which vitamin does your skin make when it is in sunlight?`,`Vitamin D`,[`Vitamin C`,`Vitamin A`,`Vitamin K`],`Sunlight on the skin helps the body make vitamin D, which keeps bones strong.`],[4,3,`body`,`What job do the kidneys do?`,`Clean waste out of the blood`,[`Pump blood around the body`,`Help you think and remember`,`Turn food into fat`],`The kidneys filter the blood and make urine to carry the waste away.`],[4,2,`body`,`Muscles can only pull; they cannot push.`,`True`,[`False`],`Muscles work in pairs: one pulls a bone one way, and its partner pulls it back.`],[5,1,`body`,`How many bones are in an adult human body?`,`206`,[`106`,`306`,`96`],`Babies are born with about 300 bones; many fuse together, leaving 206 in an adult.`],[5,2,`body`,`What is the job of red blood cells?`,`Carrying oxygen around the body`,[`Fighting germs`,`Sealing cuts`,`Making bones grow`],`Red blood cells contain haemoglobin, which picks up oxygen in the lungs and drops it off in the body.`],[5,2,`body`,`Which of these joints works like a hinge?`,`The knee`,[`The shoulder`,`The hip`,`The joins between skull bones`],`Knees and elbows bend one way like a door hinge. Shoulders and hips are ball-and-socket joints.`],[5,3,`body`,`Which part of the eye controls how much light gets in?`,`The iris`,[`The lens`,`The retina`,`The eyelash`],`The coloured iris makes the pupil bigger in dim light and smaller in bright light.`],[5,3,`body`,`Where does the digestion of food begin?`,`In the mouth`,[`In the stomach`,`In the large intestine`,`In the liver`],`Chewing and saliva start breaking food down before it is even swallowed.`],[5,1,`body`,`The heart is a muscle.`,`True`,[`False`],`The heart is made of cardiac muscle, which beats all your life without getting tired.`],[6,2,`body`,`What do white blood cells do?`,`Fight infections`,[`Carry oxygen`,`Digest fats`,`Store minerals`],`White blood cells find and destroy germs, and some make antibodies.`],[6,2,`body`,`Which tube carries food from the mouth to the stomach?`,`The oesophagus (food pipe)`,[`The windpipe (trachea)`,`The large intestine`,`An artery`],`Muscles in the oesophagus squeeze food down to the stomach, even if you are upside down.`],[6,3,`body`,`What is a balanced diet?`,`Eating the right amounts of each kind of food`,[`Eating the same food at every meal`,`Eating only fruit and vegetables`,`Eating as little as possible`],`A balanced diet has carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, minerals, fibre and water in sensible amounts.`],[6,3,`body`,`Which mineral is most important for strong bones and teeth?`,`Calcium`,[`Iron`,`Sodium`,`Iodine`],`Calcium, found in milk, cheese and leafy greens, builds bones and teeth. Iron is needed for blood.`],[6,2,`body`,`Your heart beats faster when you exercise.`,`True`,[`False`],`Working muscles need more oxygen, so the heart pumps faster to deliver more blood.`],[7,1,`body`,`Which organ in the chest exchanges oxygen and carbon dioxide with the blood?`,`The lungs`,[`The heart`,`The stomach`,`The spleen`],`Tiny air sacs called alveoli in the lungs let oxygen into the blood and carbon dioxide out.`],[7,2,`body`,`What is the function of the small air sacs (alveoli) in the lungs?`,`Gas exchange between air and blood`,[`Making sounds for speech`,`Filtering dust from food`,`Storing extra blood`],`Their huge total surface and thin walls let gases pass quickly into and out of the blood.`],[7,2,`body`,`Which part of the brain controls balance and coordination?`,`The cerebellum`,[`The cerebrum`,`The brain stem`,`The pituitary gland`],`The cerebellum, at the back of the brain, keeps movements smooth and balanced.`],[7,3,`body`,`How does a vaccine protect you?`,`It trains the immune system to recognise a germ`,[`It kills every germ in the body at once`,`It replaces your white blood cells`,`It makes your blood thicker`],`A vaccine shows the immune system a harmless form or part of a germ, so it can react fast to the real one.`],[7,3,`body`,`Which blood vessels carry blood back to the heart?`,`Veins`,[`Arteries`,`Capillaries only`,`Nerves`],`Veins return blood to the heart and have valves that stop it flowing backwards.`],[7,1,`body`,`Insulin is a hormone that helps control sugar in the blood.`,`True`,[`False`],`The pancreas releases insulin, which helps cells take in glucose. People with diabetes may need extra insulin.`],[8,1,`body`,`Which gland is often called the 'master gland' because it controls other glands?`,`The pituitary gland`,[`The thyroid gland`,`The adrenal gland`,`The sweat gland`],`The pituitary, at the base of the brain, releases hormones that tell other glands what to do.`],[8,2,`body`,`Which part of the nervous system makes very fast automatic reactions, such as pulling your hand off a hot pan?`,`A reflex arc through the spinal cord`,[`The cerebrum, after careful thought`,`The stomach and intestines`,`The lymph nodes`],`Reflexes go through the spinal cord without waiting for the brain, saving precious time.`],[8,2,`body`,`What does the hormone adrenaline do?`,`Prepares the body for 'fight or flight'`,[`Makes you fall asleep`,`Digests proteins`,`Builds new bone`],`Adrenaline raises heart rate and breathing and releases sugar for energy in a sudden emergency.`],[8,3,`body`,`Which part of the blood gathers at a cut to help it clot?`,`Platelets`,[`Red blood cells`,`White blood cells`,`Lymph`],`Platelets are tiny cell fragments that stick together at a wound and help form a clot that seals it.`],[8,3,`body`,`Which organ produces bile, which helps digest fats?`,`The liver`,[`The pancreas`,`The kidney`,`The appendix`],`The liver makes bile, which is stored in the gall bladder and released into the small intestine.`],[8,2,`body`,`Blood plasma is mostly water.`,`True`,[`False`],`Plasma, the yellowish liquid part of blood, is about nine-tenths water, with dissolved food, salts and hormones.`]],[[3,1,`matter`,`Which of these is a liquid at room temperature?`,`Milk`,[`Ice cubes`,`A wooden block`,`A stone`],`Milk flows and takes the shape of its container, so it is a liquid.`],[3,2,`matter`,`What happens to chocolate left in a very hot car?`,`It melts`,[`It freezes`,`It turns into a gas at once`,`It grows bigger`],`Heat turns solid chocolate soft and runny: it melts.`],[3,2,`matter`,`Which material is transparent (see-through)?`,`Clear glass`,[`Wood`,`Cardboard`,`Brick`],`Light passes straight through clear glass, so you can see through it.`],[3,2,`matter`,`Which of these materials is natural, not made by people?`,`Cotton`,[`Plastic`,`Nylon`,`Glass`],`Cotton comes from the cotton plant. Plastic, nylon and glass are made in factories.`],[3,3,`matter`,`Which material would be best for a raincoat?`,`Waterproof plastic`,[`Paper`,`Cotton wool`,`Tissue`],`A raincoat needs a material that water cannot soak through.`],[3,3,`matter`,`What happens when you stir sugar into warm water?`,`It dissolves and seems to disappear`,[`It turns into salt`,`It sinks and stays as lumps`,`It makes the water freeze`],`The sugar breaks into pieces too small to see and spreads through the water: it dissolves.`],[3,1,`matter`,`Air takes up space even though you cannot see it.`,`True`,[`False`],`Blow up a balloon and it gets bigger: the air inside is taking up space.`],[4,1,`matter`,`What do we call the temperature at which a solid turns into a liquid?`,`Its melting point`,[`Its boiling point`,`Its dew point`,`Its flash point`],`Each pure substance melts at its own temperature; ice melts at 0 °C.`],[4,2,`matter`,`Which of these mixtures can be separated with a sieve?`,`Pebbles mixed with sand`,[`Salt dissolved in water`,`Sugar dissolved in tea`,`Ink mixed with water`],`The sand falls through the holes of the sieve; the bigger pebbles stay behind.`],[4,2,`matter`,`Which of these is a gas?`,`The air inside a balloon`,[`The juice in a glass`,`The ice in a freezer`,`The sand in a box`],`Air is a mixture of gases. A gas spreads out to fill whatever container it is in.`],[4,3,`matter`,`What happens to most materials when they are heated?`,`They expand a little`,[`They shrink`,`They get heavier`,`They always change colour`],`Particles move faster and spread out when heated, so most materials expand. This is why bridges have small gaps.`],[4,3,`matter`,`How can you get the salt back out of salty water?`,`Let the water evaporate`,[`Pour it through a sieve`,`Shake it very hard`,`Stir in more water`],`When the water evaporates, the salt is left behind as crystals.`],[4,2,`matter`,`Wood floats on water because it is less dense than water.`,`True`,[`False`],`Most woods are less dense than water, so they float. A few very dense woods, such as ebony, sink.`],[5,1,`matter`,`What is the smallest particle of an element that still has its properties?`,`An atom`,[`A cell`,`A drop`,`A crystal`],`Everything is made of atoms. An atom of gold is still gold; split it and it is not.`],[5,2,`matter`,`Which of these is a physical change?`,`Ice melting into water`,[`Wood burning to ash`,`An egg being fried`,`Iron rusting`],`Melting changes only the state; the water is still water. The others make new substances.`],[5,2,`matter`,`In salty water, what is the salt called?`,`The solute`,[`The solvent`,`The filter`,`The residue`],`The solute (salt) dissolves in the solvent (water) to make a solution.`],[5,3,`matter`,`Which method separates sand from water?`,`Filtering`,[`Stirring`,`Using a magnet`,`Adding more water`],`Filter paper lets the water through but traps the sand grains.`],[5,3,`matter`,`Which metal is liquid at room temperature?`,`Mercury`,[`Iron`,`Copper`,`Aluminium`],`Mercury melts at about −39 °C, so it is a liquid metal at room temperature.`],[5,2,`matter`,`Gases can be squashed into a smaller space more easily than liquids.`,`True`,[`False`],`Gas particles are far apart, so they can be pushed closer together. Liquid particles are already touching.`],[6,1,`matter`,`What are substances made of only one type of atom called?`,`Elements`,[`Compounds`,`Mixtures`,`Solutions`],`Gold, oxygen and carbon are elements. Water is a compound of two elements.`],[6,2,`matter`,`What forms when two or more elements are chemically joined?`,`A compound`,[`A mixture`,`A solution`,`An element`],`In a compound the elements are bonded together, like hydrogen and oxygen in water.`],[6,2,`matter`,`What does the periodic table list?`,`All the known chemical elements`,[`All the planets in the Solar System`,`All the groups of animals`,`All the kinds of rock on Earth`],`The periodic table arranges the elements by atomic number, so similar elements line up in columns.`],[6,3,`matter`,`Which of these is an alloy?`,`Bronze`,[`Gold`,`Iron`,`Copper`],`Bronze is a mixture of copper and tin. Gold, iron and copper are pure metals (elements).`],[6,3,`matter`,`Which property describes how easily a metal can be drawn out into a wire?`,`Ductility`,[`Malleability`,`Conductivity`,`Brittleness`],`Ductile metals such as copper can be pulled into thin wires. Malleable ones can be hammered into sheets.`],[6,1,`matter`,`Iron needs both oxygen and water to rust.`,`True`,[`False`],`Iron rusts when it reacts with oxygen while water is present; in dry air it hardly rusts at all.`],[7,1,`matter`,`Sodium's symbol comes from natrium, its Latin name. What is the symbol?`,`Na`,[`So`,`Sd`,`S`],`Na comes from natrium, sodium's Latin name. S is the symbol for sulfur.`],[7,1,`matter`,`Which gas turns limewater milky?`,`Carbon dioxide`,[`Oxygen`,`Hydrogen`,`Nitrogen`],`Bubbling carbon dioxide through limewater makes it cloudy: a classic test for the gas.`],[7,1,`matter`,`What is the most common element in the universe?`,`Hydrogen`,[`Oxygen`,`Carbon`,`Iron`],`About three-quarters of the ordinary matter in the universe is hydrogen.`],[7,2,`matter`,`Which indicator turns red in acid and blue in alkali?`,`Litmus`,[`Iodine`,`Limewater`,`Copper sulfate`],`Litmus paper is a simple indicator: red for acids, blue for alkalis.`],[7,2,`matter`,`What pH is a strong acid likely to have?`,`1`,[`7`,`10`,`14`],`Acids have a pH below 7, and strong acids are around 0–2. Alkalis are above 7.`],[7,3,`matter`,`What do an acid and an alkali make when they neutralise each other?`,`A salt and water`,[`Only a gas`,`A metal and oxygen`,`Sugar and carbon dioxide`],`Neutralisation: acid + alkali → salt + water. Indigestion tablets neutralise extra stomach acid.`],[7,3,`matter`,`What are atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons called?`,`Isotopes`,[`Ions`,`Molecules`,`Compounds`],`Carbon-12 and carbon-14 are isotopes: both have 6 protons, but different numbers of neutrons.`],[7,2,`matter`,`A neutral atom has the same number of protons and electrons.`,`True`,[`False`],`Equal numbers of positive protons and negative electrons cancel out, so the atom has no overall charge.`],[8,1,`matter`,`What is the atomic number of an element?`,`The number of protons in each atom`,[`The number of neutrons in each atom`,`The total mass of each atom`,`The number of atoms in a gram`],`Every element has its own atomic number: hydrogen 1, carbon 6, oxygen 8.`],[8,1,`matter`,`What is an exothermic reaction?`,`One that gives out heat`,[`One that takes in heat`,`One that makes no change`,`One that only happens in water`],`Burning is exothermic. Reactions that take in heat, like those in instant cold packs, are endothermic.`],[8,2,`matter`,`Which type of bond holds sodium chloride together?`,`An ionic bond`,[`A covalent bond`,`A metallic bond`,`A hydrogen bond`],`Sodium gives an electron to chlorine, making oppositely charged ions that attract: an ionic bond.`],[8,2,`matter`,`What does the law of conservation of mass say?`,`Mass is neither made nor destroyed in a chemical reaction`,[`Mass always increases when things burn`,`Heavier atoms always react faster`,`Mass turns into energy in every reaction`],`The atoms are only rearranged, so the total mass of the products equals that of the reactants.`],[8,3,`matter`,`What do elements in the same group (column) of the periodic table have in common?`,`The same number of outer electrons`,[`The same atomic mass`,`The same number of neutrons`,`The same melting point`],`Elements in a group behave similarly because they have the same number of electrons in their outer shell.`],[8,3,`matter`,`Which unreactive gas glows red-orange in advertising signs?`,`Neon`,[`Oxygen`,`Chlorine`,`Nitrogen`],`Neon is a noble gas: very unreactive, and it glows when electricity passes through it.`],[8,2,`matter`,`Diamond and graphite are both made of carbon.`,`True`,[`False`],`They are different arrangements of carbon atoms: diamond is very hard, graphite is soft and slippery.`],[3,1,`energy`,`What do we call a push or a pull?`,`A force`,[`A shadow`,`A sound`,`A colour`],`Every push or pull is a force. Forces can start, stop or change how things move.`],[3,1,`energy`,`When does an object make a shadow?`,`When it blocks light`,[`When it is very cold`,`When it makes a sound`,`When it floats on water`],`Light cannot pass through the object, so a dark shape forms behind it.`],[3,2,`energy`,`What makes a sound?`,`Something vibrating`,[`Something melting`,`Something freezing`,`Something shrinking`],`Sounds are made when something moves back and forth very quickly: it vibrates.`],[3,2,`energy`,`Which of these needs electricity to work?`,`A torch`,[`A kite`,`A bicycle`,`A skipping rope`],`A torch uses electricity from its battery to make light.`],[3,3,`energy`,`Why does a ball roll farther on a smooth floor than on grass?`,`There is less friction on the smooth floor`,[`The floor pulls the ball forward`,`Grass makes the ball heavier`,`Gravity is stronger on grass`],`Rough grass rubs against the ball more, so friction slows it down sooner.`],[3,2,`energy`,`Shadows are longest around midday, when the Sun is highest.`,`False`,[`True`],`Shadows are shortest around midday and longest in the early morning and evening, when the Sun is low.`],[4,1,`energy`,`Which of these makes its own light?`,`A candle flame`,[`A mirror`,`The Moon`,`A white wall`],`A candle flame is a light source. Mirrors, the Moon and walls only reflect light.`],[4,2,`energy`,`What does a circuit need for a bulb to light up?`,`A complete loop with a power source`,[`A gap in the wire`,`Only a switch`,`A wooden connector`],`Electricity flows only around a complete, unbroken loop from the battery and back.`],[4,2,`energy`,`What happens when you open the switch in a simple circuit?`,`The circuit breaks and the bulb goes out`,[`The bulb gets brighter`,`The battery recharges`,`The current flows faster`],`An open switch makes a gap, so the current stops flowing.`],[4,3,`energy`,`Which of these is a lever?`,`A see-saw`,[`A ramp`,`A screw`,`A pulley`],`A see-saw turns about a pivot (the fulcrum), which makes it a lever.`],[4,3,`energy`,`Why do you see lightning before you hear its thunder?`,`Light travels much faster than sound`,[`Sound travels faster than light`,`Thunder starts later than lightning`,`Ears work more slowly than eyes`],`They happen at the same moment, but light arrives almost instantly while sound takes about 3 seconds per kilometre.`],[4,2,`energy`,`Like poles of two magnets pull towards each other.`,`False`,[`True`],`Like poles (north–north or south–south) push apart; opposite poles attract.`],[5,1,`energy`,`What kind of energy is stored in a stretched rubber band?`,`Elastic potential energy`,[`Sound energy`,`Light energy`,`Nuclear energy`],`Stretching stores energy in the band; letting go turns it into movement.`],[5,2,`energy`,`What does a solar panel turn sunlight into?`,`Electricity`,[`Water`,`Natural gas`,`Sound`],`Solar (photovoltaic) cells turn light energy directly into electrical energy.`],[5,2,`energy`,`Which colour of light bends the most when it passes through a prism?`,`Violet`,[`Red`,`Yellow`,`Orange`],`Violet light bends (refracts) most and red least, which spreads white light into a spectrum.`],[5,2,`energy`,`Which material would best keep a hot drink hot?`,`A plastic-foam cup`,[`A steel cup`,`A copper cup`,`An aluminium cup`],`Foam is full of trapped air, a poor conductor of heat. Metals conduct heat away quickly.`],[5,3,`energy`,`Why does a straw look bent in a glass of water?`,`Light bends as it passes from water into air`,[`Water melts the straw a little`,`The glass acts like a magnet`,`The straw gets heavier in water`],`This bending of light at the boundary between two materials is called refraction.`],[5,3,`energy`,`Which of these saves energy at home?`,`Switching off lights in empty rooms`,[`Leaving the fridge door open`,`Running a heater with the windows open`,`Leaving the TV on all night`],`Every device that is on uses energy, even when nobody is using it.`],[5,1,`energy`,`White light is a mixture of colours.`,`True`,[`False`],`A prism or raindrops split white light into the colours of the rainbow.`],[6,1,`energy`,`What is the unit of energy?`,`The joule`,[`The watt`,`The volt`,`The metre`],`Energy is measured in joules (J). A watt is one joule per second.`],[6,1,`energy`,`What is the main energy change in a battery-powered torch?`,`Chemical energy to light (and some heat)`,[`Light energy to chemical energy`,`Sound energy to light energy`,`Heat energy to chemical energy`],`The battery's stored chemical energy becomes electrical energy, which the bulb turns into light and heat.`],[6,2,`energy`,`How does heat reach Earth from the Sun across empty space?`,`By radiation`,[`By conduction`,`By convection`,`By evaporation`],`Conduction and convection need particles. Radiation (such as infrared) can cross empty space.`],[6,2,`energy`,`Why does a hot-air balloon rise?`,`Hot air is less dense than the cooler air around it`,[`Hot air is heavier than cold air`,`The flame pushes against the ground`,`The clouds pull the balloon up`],`Heating the air inside spreads it out, so the balloon is lighter than the air it pushes aside.`],[6,3,`energy`,`What does the loudness of a sound depend on?`,`The amplitude (size) of the vibrations`,[`The frequency of the vibrations only`,`The temperature of the listener`,`The colour of the vibrating object`],`Bigger vibrations carry more energy and sound louder. Frequency sets the pitch.`],[6,3,`energy`,`What does the pitch of a sound depend on?`,`The frequency of the vibrations`,[`The amplitude of the vibrations`,`The distance to the listener`,`The material of the floor`],`Faster vibrations (higher frequency) make a higher note.`],[6,2,`energy`,`Sound can travel through empty space.`,`False`,[`True`],`Sound needs particles to vibrate, so it cannot cross a vacuum. Space is silent.`],[7,1,`energy`,`What does a voltmeter measure?`,`Voltage (potential difference)`,[`Current (the flow of charge)`,`Resistance (opposition to flow)`,`Power (the rate of energy use)`],`A voltmeter is connected across a component. An ammeter, connected in line, measures current.`],[7,2,`energy`,`In a parallel circuit, what happens if one bulb breaks?`,`The other bulbs stay lit`,[`Every bulb goes out`,`The other bulbs dim for ever`,`The battery stops working`],`Each bulb in a parallel circuit has its own path, so the others keep working.`],[7,2,`energy`,`How is power worked out?`,`Energy transferred ÷ time`,[`Force × distance`,`Mass × volume`,`Current ÷ voltage`],`Power = energy transferred ÷ time (P = E ÷ t), measured in watts. Force × distance is work done.`],[7,3,`energy`,`Which energy resources formed from the remains of ancient plants and animals?`,`Fossil fuels`,[`Geothermal energy`,`Tidal energy`,`Hydroelectric energy`],`Coal, oil and gas formed over millions of years, so they are not renewable.`],[7,3,`energy`,`Which way do electrons flow through a wire connected to a battery?`,`From the negative terminal to the positive terminal`,[`From the positive terminal to the negative terminal`,`They do not move at all`,`Both ways at once all the time`],`Electrons are negative, so they move from the negative side round to the positive side. 'Conventional current' is drawn the other way.`],[7,1,`energy`,`Energy is destroyed when a machine stops.`,`False`,[`True`],`Energy is never destroyed; it is transferred, often as heat and sound that spread into the surroundings.`],[8,1,`energy`,`What is the formula for kinetic energy?`,`½ × mass × speed²`,[`mass × g × height`,`force ÷ area`,`voltage × current`],`Kinetic energy depends on mass and even more on speed: doubling the speed makes it four times as big.`],[8,1,`energy`,`What is the speed of a wave equal to?`,`Frequency × wavelength`,[`Frequency ÷ amplitude`,`Amplitude × time`,`Wavelength ÷ mass`],`v = f × λ. A 2 Hz wave with a 3 m wavelength travels at 6 m/s.`],[8,2,`energy`,`Which formula gives gravitational potential energy?`,`mass × g × height`,[`½ × mass × speed²`,`force × time`,`current × resistance`],`Lifting a mass higher stores more energy: E = m × g × h, with g about 9.8 N/kg on Earth.`],[8,2,`energy`,`What happens to the resistance of a wire as it gets longer?`,`It increases`,[`It decreases`,`It stays the same`,`It becomes zero`],`A longer wire gives the moving electrons more collisions, so its resistance rises.`],[8,3,`energy`,`A machine turns 200 J of energy into 50 J of useful work. How efficient is it?`,`25 %`,[`50 %`,`75 %`,`400 %`],`Efficiency = useful output ÷ input = 50 ÷ 200 = 0.25, or 25 %.`],[8,3,`energy`,`What kind of wave is light?`,`A transverse electromagnetic wave`,[`A longitudinal sound wave`,`A water wave that needs a medium`,`A wave of moving air particles`],`Light is an electromagnetic wave: it needs no medium and its vibrations are at right angles to its direction.`],[8,2,`energy`,`Pressure equals force divided by area.`,`True`,[`False`],`A sharp knife cuts well because its force acts on a tiny area, giving a large pressure.`]],[[3,1,`earth`,`What falls from clouds when their water drops get too heavy?`,`Rain`,[`Sand`,`Smoke`,`Leaves`],`Tiny droplets in a cloud join together until they are heavy enough to fall as rain.`],[3,1,`earth`,`What is soil made of?`,`Bits of rock mixed with rotted plants and animals`,[`Only tiny grains of sand and nothing else`,`Melted metal that has cooled down`,`Dried paint and pieces of glass`],`Soil forms over many years as rock breaks down and mixes with decayed living things.`],[3,2,`earth`,`What does a thermometer measure?`,`Temperature`,[`Rainfall`,`Wind direction`,`Time`],`A thermometer shows how hot or cold something is.`],[3,2,`earth`,`Which of these is a natural resource?`,`Water`,[`Plastic`,`Glass`,`Concrete`],`Natural resources come from nature, such as water, air, soil and trees. Plastic, glass and concrete are made by people.`],[3,3,`earth`,`Why is recycling helpful?`,`It saves resources and cuts down on waste`,[`It makes more rubbish`,`It uses up extra energy for nothing`,`It fills up landfill sites faster`],`Recycling turns old materials into new things, so less is dug up and less is thrown away.`],[3,3,`earth`,`What shows which way the wind is blowing?`,`A wind vane`,[`A rain gauge`,`A thermometer`,`A sundial`],`A wind vane swings round to point into the wind, showing the direction it comes from.`],[3,2,`earth`,`Clouds are made of tiny drops of water or bits of ice.`,`True`,[`False`],`When water vapour cools high up, it turns into tiny droplets or ice crystals that we see as clouds.`],[4,1,`earth`,`What do we call water that falls from the sky as rain, snow or hail?`,`Precipitation`,[`Evaporation`,`Condensation`,`Erosion`],`Any water falling from clouds to the ground is precipitation.`],[4,2,`earth`,`What is erosion?`,`The wearing away of land by water, wind or ice`,[`Rock melting deep underground`,`Plants growing on bare land`,`Rain falling on a city`],`Rivers, waves, wind and glaciers slowly carry rock and soil away.`],[4,2,`earth`,`Which type of rock forms from layers of sand and mud pressed together?`,`Sedimentary rock`,[`Igneous rock`,`Metamorphic rock`,`Volcanic glass`],`Sandstone and limestone are sedimentary: layers settle, are pressed down and harden over time.`],[4,3,`earth`,`What do we call a sudden shaking of the ground?`,`An earthquake`,[`A tornado`,`A drought`,`A landslide`],`Earthquakes happen when rocks underground suddenly slip along a crack called a fault.`],[4,3,`earth`,`Why is it hotter in summer than in winter?`,`The Sun is higher in the sky and the days are longer`,[`Earth is much closer to the Sun in summer`,`The Sun burns more fuel in summer`,`Clouds trap all the heat in summer`],`Earth's tilt makes the Sun climb higher and stay up longer in summer. The change in Earth's distance from the Sun is tiny.`],[4,1,`earth`,`Most of Earth's surface is covered by water.`,`True`,[`False`],`About 71 % of Earth's surface is ocean, lakes, rivers and ice.`],[5,1,`earth`,`What do we call molten rock that is still underground?`,`Magma`,[`Lava`,`Granite`,`Sediment`],`Molten rock underground is magma. When it pours out of a volcano it is called lava.`],[5,2,`earth`,`What powers the water cycle?`,`Heat from the Sun`,[`Light from the Moon`,`Wind from the ocean`,`Heat from Earth's core`],`The Sun's heat evaporates water, which rises, cools into clouds and falls again as precipitation.`],[5,2,`earth`,`What is humidity?`,`The amount of water vapour in the air`,[`The speed of the wind`,`The weight of the clouds`,`The temperature of the sea`],`Humid air holds a lot of water vapour, which is why sweat dries slowly on a humid day.`],[5,3,`earth`,`Which layer of the Earth is the hottest?`,`The inner core`,[`The crust`,`The upper mantle`,`The atmosphere`],`The inner core is solid iron and nickel at around 5,000 °C or more, kept solid by enormous pressure.`],[5,3,`earth`,`What is a tsunami?`,`A huge sea wave, often caused by an undersea earthquake`,[`A strong spinning wind over land`,`A long dry spell without rain`,`A snowstorm high in the mountains`],`When the sea floor suddenly moves, it pushes up a vast volume of water that races across the ocean.`],[5,2,`earth`,`A weather forecast tells you exactly what the weather will be.`,`False`,[`True`],`Forecasts are predictions based on measurements and computer models. They are often right, but never certain.`],[6,1,`earth`,`Which type of rock forms when existing rock is changed by heat and pressure?`,`Metamorphic rock`,[`Sedimentary rock`,`Igneous rock`,`Pumice`],`Marble forms from limestone and slate from mudstone under heat and pressure, without melting.`],[6,2,`earth`,`What is the layer of gases around Earth called?`,`The atmosphere`,[`The lithosphere`,`The hydrosphere`,`The mantle`],`The atmosphere holds the air we breathe and shields us from much harmful radiation.`],[6,2,`earth`,`What is the difference between weather and climate?`,`Weather is day to day; climate is the usual pattern over many years`,[`They mean exactly the same thing`,`Weather is about the sea; climate is about land`,`Climate changes every hour; weather never changes`],`A rainy day is weather. A place that is usually wet in summer has that climate.`],[6,3,`earth`,`What instrument measures air pressure?`,`A barometer`,[`A hygrometer`,`An anemometer`,`A seismometer`],`Falling pressure on a barometer often means unsettled, wetter weather is coming.`],[6,3,`earth`,`Which instrument records the shaking of earthquakes?`,`A seismometer`,[`A barometer`,`A thermometer`,`A spectroscope`],`Seismometers detect ground movement; their records are called seismograms.`],[6,1,`earth`,`Coal, oil and natural gas are renewable.`,`False`,[`True`],`They took millions of years to form and are being used much faster, so they will run out.`],[7,1,`earth`,`What scale is often used to compare the strength of earthquakes?`,`The magnitude (Richter) scale`,[`The Beaufort wind scale`,`The pH acidity scale`,`The Celsius temperature scale`],`Each whole step up in magnitude means roughly 32 times more energy released.`],[7,2,`earth`,`Which scale describes wind speed by its effects, from calm to hurricane?`,`The Beaufort scale`,[`The Richter scale`,`The Mohs scale`,`The Kelvin scale`],`Force 0 is calm; force 12 is hurricane force.`],[7,2,`earth`,`On the Mohs scale, which mineral is the hardest?`,`Diamond`,[`Talc`,`Quartz`,`Gypsum`],`The Mohs scale runs from talc (1, softest) to diamond (10, hardest).`],[7,3,`earth`,`What is the greenhouse effect?`,`Gases in the atmosphere trapping heat near Earth's surface`,[`Plants growing faster inside glass houses`,`Holes in the ground releasing hot steam`,`The Sun shining more brightly each year`],`Some greenhouse effect keeps Earth warm enough for life; adding more of these gases makes it warmer still.`],[7,3,`earth`,`Why are coral reefs at risk when oceans get warmer?`,`Heat makes corals lose the algae that feed them (bleaching)`,[`Warm water freezes the corals solid`,`Fish stop swimming near warm reefs`,`Warm water turns the coral into sand at once`],`Stressed corals expel their algae and turn white; if it lasts too long they die.`],[7,2,`earth`,`The ozone layer helps protect life from harmful ultraviolet light.`,`True`,[`False`],`Ozone high in the atmosphere absorbs much of the Sun's UV radiation.`],[8,1,`earth`,`Which gas, released by burning fossil fuels, is the main cause of recent climate change?`,`Carbon dioxide`,[`Oxygen`,`Argon`,`Helium`],`Burning coal, oil and gas adds carbon dioxide, which traps extra heat in the atmosphere.`],[8,2,`earth`,`What happens at a convergent plate boundary between an ocean plate and a continental plate?`,`The ocean plate sinks beneath the continent (subduction)`,[`The two plates slide apart and leave a gap`,`The plates melt completely into the sea`,`Nothing, because plates never touch`],`Subduction makes deep trenches and volcanoes, such as the Andes and the volcanoes of Japan.`],[8,3,`earth`,`What does carbon dating measure?`,`How long ago a once-living thing died`,[`How hot a rock is inside`,`How much carbon a car gives off`,`How deep a fossil is buried`],`Carbon-14 decays at a steady rate, so the amount left shows the age of remains up to about 50,000 years.`],[8,3,`earth`,`What is the water table?`,`The level below which the ground is full of water`,[`A table that measures rainfall`,`The top surface of the sea`,`A map of all the rivers`],`Wells must reach below the water table to find water.`],[8,2,`earth`,`Earth's continents are still moving today.`,`True`,[`False`],`They move a few centimetres a year, about as fast as your fingernails grow.`],[3,1,`space`,`What do we call Earth's natural satellite?`,`The Moon`,[`The Sun`,`Mars`,`A comet`],`The Moon travels around Earth. It is our only natural satellite.`],[3,1,`space`,`When can we usually see the stars?`,`At night, when the sky is dark`,[`Only at noon, when the Sun is high`,`Only on cloudy days`,`Only during a thunderstorm`],`Stars are in the sky all the time, but the bright daytime sky hides them.`],[3,2,`space`,`Which planet do we live on?`,`Earth`,[`Mars`,`Venus`,`Saturn`],`We live on Earth, the third planet from the Sun.`],[3,2,`space`,`Why does the Moon seem to shine at night?`,`It reflects light from the Sun`,[`It makes its own light`,`It is on fire`,`It is covered in lamps`],`The Moon has no light of its own: it reflects sunlight back to us.`],[3,3,`space`,`About how long does Earth take to travel once around the Sun?`,`About one year`,[`One day`,`One week`,`One month`],`Earth takes about 365¼ days to orbit the Sun, which is why we have leap years.`],[3,3,`space`,`Stars seem to twinkle because the air above us is always moving.`,`True`,[`False`],`Moving air bends starlight back and forth a little. Seen from space, stars do not twinkle.`],[4,1,`space`,`Which planet is closest to the Sun?`,`Mercury`,[`Venus`,`Earth`,`Mars`],`Mercury is the smallest planet and orbits closest to the Sun.`],[4,2,`space`,`What is a group of stars that makes a pattern in the sky called?`,`A constellation`,[`A galaxy`,`A comet`,`An orbit`],`Orion and the Plough (Big Dipper) are well-known star patterns, or constellations.`],[4,2,`space`,`Which planet has the most famous bright rings?`,`Saturn`,[`Mars`,`Mercury`,`Venus`],`Saturn's rings are made of ice and rock. Jupiter, Uranus and Neptune have much fainter rings.`],[4,3,`space`,`What covers the surface of the Moon?`,`Dust, rocks and craters`,[`Oceans and forests`,`Thick clouds`,`Grass and sand dunes`],`The Moon has no air or liquid water. Its surface is grey dust and rock dotted with impact craters.`],[4,3,`space`,`Which tool helps us see faraway planets and stars more clearly?`,`A telescope`,[`A microscope`,`A periscope`,`A stethoscope`],`Telescopes collect more light than our eyes and magnify distant objects.`],[4,1,`space`,`The Sun is much bigger than the Moon.`,`True`,[`False`],`The Sun is about 400 times wider than the Moon; it only looks similar in size because it is about 400 times farther away.`],[5,1,`space`,`What is the name of our galaxy?`,`The Milky Way`,[`Andromeda`,`The Solar Wind`,`Orion`],`Our Sun is one of hundreds of billions of stars in the Milky Way.`],[5,2,`space`,`Which planet is the hottest?`,`Venus`,[`Mercury`,`Mars`,`Jupiter`],`Venus's thick carbon dioxide atmosphere traps heat, making it hotter than Mercury, which is closer to the Sun.`],[5,2,`space`,`Which force keeps the planets in orbit around the Sun?`,`Gravity`,[`Magnetism`,`Friction`,`Air resistance`],`The Sun's gravity pulls on each planet, bending its path into an orbit.`],[5,3,`space`,`What causes a solar eclipse?`,`The Moon passing between the Sun and Earth`,[`Earth passing between the Sun and the Moon`,`Clouds covering the Sun`,`The Sun switching off for a while`],`The Moon's shadow falls on part of Earth and blocks the Sun from view there.`],[5,3,`space`,`What is a comet mostly made of?`,`Ice and dust`,[`Burning gas`,`Solid iron`,`Liquid water`],`Near the Sun a comet's ice turns to gas, making a glowing head and a long tail.`],[5,1,`space`,`A day on Mars is about as long as a day on Earth.`,`True`,[`False`],`Mars spins once in about 24 hours 37 minutes, very close to Earth's 24 hours.`],[6,1,`space`,`What is a light-year a measure of?`,`Distance`,[`Time`,`Brightness`,`Weight`],`A light-year is how far light travels in a year: about 9.5 trillion kilometres.`],[6,2,`space`,`What are the four inner, rocky planets?`,`Mercury, Venus, Earth and Mars`,[`Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune`,`Mars, Jupiter, Saturn and Pluto`,`Venus, Earth, Saturn and Neptune`],`The inner planets are small and rocky; the outer four are giants made mostly of gas and ice.`],[6,2,`space`,`Why do astronauts appear to float on a space station?`,`They and the station are falling around Earth together`,[`There is no gravity at all in space`,`Their spacesuits are filled with helium`,`The station's magnets hold them up`],`Gravity is still strong there, but everything falls together in orbit, so nothing presses on anything.`],[6,3,`space`,`Where is the asteroid belt?`,`Between Mars and Jupiter`,[`Between Earth and Mars`,`Beyond Neptune`,`Between Venus and Earth`],`Most asteroids orbit the Sun in a broad belt between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter.`],[6,3,`space`,`Why does the Moon always show us the same face?`,`It spins once in the same time it takes to orbit Earth`,[`It does not spin at all`,`Earth's clouds hide the other side`,`The far side is always dark`],`This is called tidal locking. The far side does get sunlight; we just never see it from Earth.`],[6,1,`space`,`Pluto is now classed as a dwarf planet.`,`True`,[`False`],`In 2006 astronomers grouped Pluto with similar small, icy worlds as a dwarf planet.`],[7,1,`space`,`What is the Sun mostly made of?`,`Hydrogen and helium`,[`Iron and nickel`,`Oxygen and nitrogen`,`Rock and ice`],`The Sun is a ball of hot gas, about three-quarters hydrogen and one-quarter helium.`],[7,2,`space`,`What powers the Sun?`,`Nuclear fusion of hydrogen into helium`,[`Burning coal deep inside it`,`Electricity from the planets`,`Chemical batteries in its core`],`In the Sun's core, hydrogen nuclei fuse into helium, releasing huge amounts of energy.`],[7,2,`space`,`What mainly causes ocean tides on Earth?`,`The Moon's gravity`,[`The wind blowing over the sea`,`Earth's magnetic field`,`Rivers flowing into the sea`],`The Moon pulls on Earth's oceans, making bulges; the Sun adds a smaller pull.`],[7,3,`space`,`What is a black hole?`,`A region where gravity is so strong that not even light can escape`,[`A dark planet that has no moons or rings at all`,`A hole in Earth's ozone layer above the South Pole`,`An empty stretch of space between two galaxies`],`Black holes can form when very massive stars collapse at the end of their lives.`],[7,2,`space`,`Light from the Sun takes about 8 minutes to reach Earth.`,`True`,[`False`],`Sunlight travels about 150 million kilometres in roughly 8 minutes 20 seconds.`],[8,1,`space`,`What is a red giant?`,`A huge, cooler star near the end of its life`,[`A planet covered in red dust`,`A newly forming star`,`A comet with a red tail`],`When a star like the Sun runs low on hydrogen in its core, it swells into a red giant.`],[8,2,`space`,`Why is the sky blue on a clear day?`,`Air scatters blue light from the Sun more than red light`,[`The sky reflects the colour of the oceans`,`Blue gas is mixed into the air`,`The Sun gives out only blue light`],`Short blue wavelengths are scattered across the sky by air molecules; this is called Rayleigh scattering.`],[8,2,`space`,`What does the Hubble Space Telescope do that ground telescopes find harder?`,`It sees space without the blurring of Earth's atmosphere`,[`It lands on distant planets`,`It brings back rocks from the Moon`,`It controls the weather`],`Orbiting above the air, Hubble has taken extremely sharp images since its launch in 1990.`],[8,3,`space`,`What evidence suggests the universe is expanding?`,`Light from distant galaxies is shifted towards red`,[`The Moon is getting closer to Earth`,`Stars twinkle more each year`,`The Sun is shrinking quickly`],`The farther away a galaxy is, the more its light is redshifted: galaxies are moving apart.`],[8,3,`space`,`What is a nebula?`,`A huge cloud of gas and dust in space`,[`A small, rocky moon`,`The bright centre of a comet`,`A type of rocket engine`],`New stars form inside nebulae, where gravity pulls the gas and dust together.`],[8,1,`space`,`Jupiter has more mass than all the other planets put together.`,`True`,[`False`],`Jupiter's mass is about two and a half times that of all the other planets combined.`]],[[3,1,`living`,`What do most caterpillars eat?`,`Leaves`,[`Small fish`,`Tree bark only`,`Other caterpillars`],`Most caterpillars munch leaves, often from just a few kinds of plant.`],[3,1,`living`,`Which animal carries a shell that protects its soft body?`,`A snail`,[`A worm`,`A slug`,`A frog`],`A snail can pull its body inside its coiled shell. A slug is similar but has no outer shell.`],[3,1,`living`,`What is a baby cow called?`,`A calf`,[`A foal`,`A kid`,`A cub`],`A baby cow is a calf. A foal is a young horse, a kid a young goat and a cub a young bear, lion or fox.`],[3,2,`living`,`Which part of a plant holds it up and carries water to the leaves?`,`The stem`,[`The petal`,`The seed`,`The pollen`],`The stem supports the plant and has tubes inside that carry water up from the roots.`],[3,2,`living`,`Besides water, what do plants take from the soil?`,`Nutrients (minerals)`,[`Sugar`,`Light`,`Oxygen bubbles`],`Roots take up nutrients (minerals) such as nitrogen. Plants make their own sugar using sunlight.`],[3,2,`living`,`Which insect lives in a hive and makes honey?`,`The honeybee`,[`The ant`,`The beetle`,`The dragonfly`],`Honeybees live in colonies in hives. Ants live in nests, not hives.`],[3,3,`living`,`Why do cacti have spines instead of broad, flat leaves?`,`To lose less water and to keep animals away`,[`To catch rain like a cup`,`To make more seeds each year`,`To soak up sunlight faster`],`Thin spines lose far less water than broad leaves, which matters in a dry desert.`],[3,2,`living`,`Some plants can catch and digest insects.`,`True`,[`False`],`The Venus flytrap and the pitcher plant trap insects to get extra nutrients from poor soil.`],[4,1,`living`,`What do we call an animal that eats both plants and meat?`,`An omnivore`,[`A herbivore`,`A carnivore`,`A producer`],`Bears, crows and humans are omnivores: they eat plant and animal foods.`],[4,1,`living`,`Which of these senses does the skin give you?`,`Touch`,[`Sight`,`Hearing`,`Smell`],`Nerve endings in the skin sense touch, pressure, heat, cold and pain.`],[4,2,`living`,`Which of these is a producer in a food chain?`,`Grass`,[`A rabbit`,`A hawk`,`A mushroom`],`Grass makes its own food from sunlight. A mushroom is a decomposer, not a producer.`],[4,2,`living`,`What do we call animals that feed on dead animals they did not kill?`,`Scavengers`,[`Predators`,`Producers`,`Pollinators`],`Vultures and hyenas are well-known scavengers that help clean up an ecosystem.`],[4,3,`living`,`How do fish take in oxygen from water?`,`Through their gills`,[`Through their fins`,`Through their scales`,`Through their tails`],`Water flows over the gills, where oxygen passes into the fish's blood.`],[4,3,`living`,`Why do some animals hibernate?`,`To survive winter when food is scarce`,[`To grow a new skin each spring`,`To lay eggs in deep snow`,`To keep cool in hot summers`],`During hibernation an animal's body slows right down, so it needs very little energy.`],[4,2,`living`,`Every plant grows from a seed.`,`False`,[`True`],`Ferns and mosses grow from tiny spores instead of seeds.`],[5,1,`living`,`Which of these mammals lays eggs?`,`The platypus`,[`The bat`,`The whale`,`The kangaroo`],`The duck-billed platypus and the echidnas are the only mammals that lay eggs.`],[5,2,`living`,`What do we call it when a seed starts to grow?`,`Germination`,[`Pollination`,`Photosynthesis`,`Evaporation`],`A seed needs water, warmth and air to germinate; the root usually appears first.`],[5,2,`living`,`Which group of animals has scales, fins and gills?`,`Fish`,[`Amphibians`,`Reptiles`,`Birds`],`Fish live in water, breathe with gills and swim with fins.`],[5,2,`living`,`What is a group of lions called?`,`A pride`,[`A pack`,`A flock`,`A swarm`],`Lions are the only big cats that live in groups, called prides.`],[5,3,`living`,`What is a parasite?`,`A living thing that lives on or in another and harms it`,[`An animal that eats only plants`,`A plant that makes its food from light`,`A fungus that breaks down dead leaves`],`Fleas, ticks and tapeworms are parasites: they feed on their host.`],[5,3,`living`,`Why do deserts have few large trees?`,`There is too little water for them`,[`The soil is far too rich`,`There is too much shade`,`It rains there every day`],`Big trees need a lot of water, so desert plants are usually small, spiny or succulent.`],[5,1,`living`,`In science, a tomato counts as a fruit.`,`True`,[`False`],`A fruit is the part of a plant that holds the seeds, so tomatoes, cucumbers and peppers are fruits.`],[6,1,`living`,`Which of these is found in plant cells but not in animal cells?`,`A cell wall`,[`A nucleus`,`A cell membrane`,`Cytoplasm`],`Plant cells have a tough cell wall (and chloroplasts); animal cells have only a membrane.`],[6,1,`living`,`Which of these animals is an insect?`,`A beetle`,[`A spider`,`A centipede`,`A crab`],`Beetles have six legs and three body parts. Spiders, centipedes and crabs are other kinds of arthropod.`],[6,2,`living`,`What does the cell membrane do?`,`Controls what enters and leaves the cell`,[`Makes food from sunlight`,`Stores the genetic information`,`Gives the plant its green colour`],`The membrane is a thin, flexible layer that lets some substances through and keeps others out.`],[6,2,`living`,`What are the male and female parts of a flower called?`,`The stamen and the carpel`,[`The sepal and the petal`,`The root and the stem`,`The pollen and the nectar`],`The stamen makes pollen; the carpel (pistil) holds the ovules that become seeds.`],[6,2,`living`,`Which plant tissue carries water up from the roots?`,`Xylem`,[`Phloem`,`Cuticle`,`Stoma`],`Xylem tubes carry water and minerals up; phloem carries sugars around the plant.`],[6,3,`living`,`Which two groups of animals are warm-blooded?`,`Birds and mammals`,[`Reptiles and amphibians`,`Fish and reptiles`,`Insects and fish`],`Warm-blooded animals keep a steady body temperature using energy from food.`],[6,2,`living`,`Some bacteria are useful, for example in making yoghurt.`,`True`,[`False`],`Helpful bacteria turn milk into yoghurt and live in our gut, where they help digestion.`],[7,1,`living`,`What is the largest animal that has ever lived?`,`The blue whale`,[`The African elephant`,`Tyrannosaurus rex`,`The giant squid`],`A blue whale can be about 30 metres long, bigger than any known dinosaur.`],[7,1,`living`,`What is the word for all the members of one species living in one area?`,`A population`,[`A community`,`An ecosystem`,`A biome`],`All the rabbits in a field form a population; all the species there together form a community.`],[7,1,`living`,`What do we call animals whose body temperature changes with their surroundings?`,`Cold-blooded (ectothermic)`,[`Warm-blooded (endothermic)`,`Nocturnal`,`Domesticated`],`Lizards bask in the sun to warm up because their bodies do not make much heat.`],[7,2,`living`,`In ecology, what is a community?`,`All the populations of different species in an area`,[`One species living on its own`,`Only the plants growing in a field`,`The rocks and soil of a habitat`],`A pond community includes its fish, insects, plants, algae and microbes.`],[7,2,`living`,`What is the carrying capacity of a habitat?`,`The largest population of a species it can support`,[`The number of rivers flowing through it`,`The total weight of its soil`,`How fast its plants come into flower`],`Food, water and space limit how many individuals a habitat can keep alive.`],[7,3,`living`,`What is natural selection?`,`Living things better suited to their surroundings survive and breed more`,[`Animals choosing their own colours`,`Farmers picking out the best crops`,`Living things changing on purpose to fit in`],`Over many generations, helpful features become more common in a population.`],[7,3,`living`,`What do we call a group of similar cells working together?`,`A tissue`,[`An organ system`,`A molecule`,`A population`],`Cells form tissues, tissues form organs, and organs work together in organ systems.`],[7,1,`living`,`Members of a species can breed together and have young that can also breed.`,`True`,[`False`],`A horse and a donkey can have a mule, but mules cannot usually breed, so horses and donkeys are separate species.`],[8,1,`living`,`What are the building blocks of proteins?`,`Amino acids`,[`Fatty acids`,`Glucose molecules`,`Nucleotides`],`Twenty kinds of amino acid join in long chains to make every protein in the body.`],[8,1,`living`,`Who devised the system of giving each species a two-part Latin name?`,`Carl Linnaeus`,[`Charles Darwin`,`Gregor Mendel`,`Louis Pasteur`],`In Linnaeus's system humans are Homo sapiens: genus first, then species.`],[8,2,`living`,`What is a dominant allele?`,`One whose feature shows even when only one copy is present`,[`One that always skips a generation`,`One found only in males`,`One that is never passed on`],`A recessive allele's feature shows only when two copies are inherited.`],[8,2,`living`,`What is homeostasis?`,`Keeping conditions inside the body steady`,[`Growing new body cells`,`Fighting off a disease`,`Digesting food quickly`],`Body temperature, water and blood sugar are all kept within narrow limits.`],[8,3,`living`,`Which kind of cell division makes sex cells with half the usual number of chromosomes?`,`Meiosis`,[`Mitosis`,`Fertilisation`,`Respiration`],`Meiosis halves the chromosome number, so a fertilised egg ends up with the full set.`],[8,3,`living`,`Why are fossils of sea creatures sometimes found high up on mountains?`,`The rock formed under the sea and was later pushed up`,[`Sea creatures used to climb mountains`,`Birds carried the shells up there`,`A flood carried them uphill last year`],`Moving plates can lift old sea-floor rock thousands of metres, as in the Himalayas.`],[8,1,`living`,`Identical twins have the same DNA.`,`True`,[`False`],`Identical twins come from one fertilised egg that split in two, so their DNA is the same apart from tiny changes.`],[3,1,`body`,`Which body parts help you hear?`,`The ears`,[`The eyes`,`The toes`,`The teeth`],`Your ears collect sound waves, and your brain turns the signals into sounds you recognise.`],[3,2,`body`,`What should you do after sneezing into your hands?`,`Wash your hands`,[`Clap your hands`,`Touch your face`,`Shake hands with friends`],`Washing stops the germs from your sneeze spreading to other people and things.`],[3,2,`body`,`Why should you brush your teeth twice a day?`,`To remove food and germs that cause decay`,[`To make your teeth grow longer`,`To change their colour`,`To keep your tongue warm`],`Bacteria feed on bits of food and make acid that damages teeth.`],[3,3,`body`,`Which of these is the healthiest everyday snack?`,`An apple`,[`A bag of sweets`,`A fizzy drink`,`A chocolate bar`],`Fruit gives you vitamins and fibre without lots of added sugar.`],[3,2,`body`,`Exercise helps make your heart and muscles stronger.`,`True`,[`False`],`Regular activity trains your heart and muscles, just as practice trains your brain.`],[4,1,`body`,`What protects your brain?`,`Your skull`,[`Your ribs`,`Your spine`,`Your kneecaps`],`The skull is a hard case of bone around the brain.`],[4,2,`body`,`What protects your heart and lungs?`,`Your rib cage`,[`Your skull`,`Your pelvis`,`Your shoulder blades only`],`The ribs form a cage of bone around the chest.`],[4,2,`body`,`Which foods give you quick energy?`,`Carbohydrates, like rice and bread`,[`Proteins, like eggs and fish`,`Vitamins, like vitamin C`,`Minerals, like calcium`],`Carbohydrates break down into sugar, which cells use for energy.`],[4,3,`body`,`What happens to your pupils in a dark room?`,`They get bigger`,[`They get smaller`,`They turn red`,`They close completely`],`Wider pupils let in more of the little light there is.`],[4,1,`body`,`Germs are too small to see without a microscope.`,`True`,[`False`],`Bacteria and viruses are microscopic; you need a microscope to see them.`],[5,1,`body`,`Which organ lets you think, feel and remember?`,`The brain`,[`The stomach`,`The liver`,`The lungs`],`The brain controls the body and holds your thoughts, feelings and memories.`],[5,2,`body`,`Which part of the digestive system takes most water back out of waste food?`,`The large intestine`,[`The mouth`,`The oesophagus`,`The stomach`],`By the time food reaches the large intestine, it is mostly water being taken back into the body.`],[5,2,`body`,`What does saliva do?`,`Starts breaking down food and makes it easier to swallow`,[`Cleans waste out of the blood`,`Carries oxygen to the muscles`,`Builds new bone in the jaw`],`Saliva contains an enzyme that starts breaking down starch.`],[5,3,`body`,`Why do we shiver when we are cold?`,`Shaking muscles make heat`,[`To dry the skin faster`,`To use up spare oxygen`,`To slow the heart down`],`Rapid small muscle movements release heat that warms the body.`],[5,2,`body`,`Your fingerprints are unique to you.`,`True`,[`False`],`No two people, not even identical twins, have been found with the same fingerprints.`],[6,1,`body`,`What is the voice box called?`,`The larynx`,[`The pharynx`,`The trachea`,`The epiglottis`],`Vocal cords in the larynx vibrate as air passes over them, making the sound of your voice.`],[6,1,`body`,`Which body system includes the heart, blood and blood vessels?`,`The circulatory system`,[`The digestive system`,`The nervous system`,`The respiratory system`],`The circulatory system carries oxygen, food and waste around the body.`],[6,2,`body`,`What stops food going down your windpipe when you swallow?`,`The epiglottis`,[`The tonsils`,`The uvula`,`The vocal cords`],`The epiglottis is a flap that covers the windpipe each time you swallow.`],[6,2,`body`,`What are the three main kinds of muscle?`,`Skeletal, smooth and cardiac`,[`Big, medium and small`,`Fast, slow and still`,`Arm, leg and back`],`Skeletal muscles move bones, smooth muscles line organs, and cardiac muscle is the heart.`],[6,3,`body`,`Which nutrient keeps food moving through the gut and prevents constipation?`,`Fibre`,[`Fat`,`Salt`,`Sugar`],`Fibre from vegetables, fruit and whole grains adds bulk that helps the gut push food along.`],[6,3,`body`,`Teenagers usually need more sleep than adults.`,`True`,[`False`],`Health experts suggest about 8–10 hours a night for teenagers and about 7–9 for adults.`],[7,1,`body`,`What are the tiny blood vessels where substances pass to and from cells called?`,`Capillaries`,[`Arteries`,`Veins`,`Nerves`],`Capillary walls are only one cell thick, so oxygen and food can pass through easily.`],[7,1,`body`,`Smoking damages the lungs.`,`True`,[`False`],`Tar and chemicals in smoke damage the airways and air sacs and can cause cancer.`],[7,2,`body`,`How many chambers does the human heart have?`,`Four`,[`Two`,`Three`,`Six`],`Two atria receive blood and two ventricles pump it out.`],[7,2,`body`,`Which side of the heart pumps blood to the lungs?`,`The right side`,[`The left side`,`Both sides equally`,`Neither side`],`The right side sends blood to the lungs; the stronger left side pumps it round the whole body.`],[7,2,`body`,`Which body system includes the lungs and the windpipe?`,`The respiratory system`,[`The excretory system`,`The skeletal system`,`The endocrine system`],`The respiratory system brings air in and out so gases can be exchanged.`],[7,3,`body`,`What is the job of the diaphragm?`,`It helps you breathe by changing the size of the chest`,[`It pumps blood out to the arms and legs`,`It digests proteins in the stomach`,`It filters waste out of the blood`],`When the diaphragm tightens and flattens, the chest gets bigger and air rushes into the lungs.`],[8,2,`body`,`What does the thyroid gland control?`,`How fast the body uses energy (metabolism)`,[`How tall the bones can grow`,`How fast the hair grows`,`How sharp the eyesight is`],`Thyroid hormones set the pace of the body's chemical reactions.`],[8,2,`body`,`What is an antibody?`,`A protein made by white blood cells that targets a germ`,[`A medicine that kills every virus`,`A kind of red blood cell`,`A germ that causes a disease`],`Each kind of antibody fits a particular germ, marking it for destruction.`],[8,3,`body`,`Why does the body need iron?`,`To make haemoglobin, which carries oxygen in the blood`,[`To make bones and teeth hard and strong`,`To help the liver digest fats in food`,`To build the hair and the nails`],`Too little iron causes anaemia, which makes people tired and pale.`],[8,3,`body`,`What causes type 1 diabetes?`,`The pancreas makes little or no insulin`,[`Catching it from another person`,`Too little vitamin D`,`Drinking too much water`],`Without enough insulin, glucose builds up in the blood, so people with type 1 diabetes need insulin treatment.`],[8,2,`body`,`Nerve cells (neurones) carry electrical signals.`,`True`,[`False`],`Neurones pass messages as electrical impulses, and chemicals carry them across the gaps between cells.`]],[[3,1,`matter`,`Which of these is a solid?`,`A pencil`,[`Orange juice`,`Steam`,`Air`],`A solid keeps its own shape. Liquids flow and gases spread out.`],[3,2,`matter`,`Which material bends and stretches most easily?`,`Rubber`,[`Glass`,`Stone`,`Brick`],`Rubber is flexible and elastic, which is why it is used for bands, tyres and balls.`],[3,3,`matter`,`Why are cooking pans often made of metal?`,`Metal lets heat pass through it easily`,[`Metal is lighter than air`,`Metal keeps food cold`,`Metal makes food taste sweeter`],`Metals are good conductors of heat, so the heat from the cooker reaches the food quickly.`],[4,1,`matter`,`What do we call a material that does not let any light through?`,`Opaque`,[`Transparent`,`Translucent`,`Reflective`],`Wood and metal are opaque. Glass is transparent; frosted glass is translucent.`],[4,2,`matter`,`Frosted glass lets some light through, but you cannot see clearly through it. It is…`,`Translucent`,[`Opaque`,`Transparent`,`Magnetic`],`Translucent materials scatter light as it passes through, so shapes look blurred.`],[4,2,`matter`,`Which of these changes can be reversed?`,`Melting butter`,[`Burning paper`,`Cooking an egg`,`A nail rusting`],`Melted butter sets again when it cools. Burning, cooking and rusting make new substances.`],[4,3,`matter`,`What forms on a cold window when warm, damp air touches it?`,`Tiny water droplets`,[`Specks of salt`,`Grains of dust`,`Small ice cubes in summer`],`Water vapour in the air cools on the glass and condenses into droplets.`],[4,2,`matter`,`Ice floats because it is less dense than liquid water.`,`True`,[`False`],`Water expands when it freezes, so ice is lighter than the same volume of water and floats.`],[5,1,`matter`,`Which of these is a mixture?`,`Air`,[`Pure water`,`Oxygen`,`Gold`],`Air is a mixture of gases, mostly nitrogen and oxygen, that are not chemically joined.`],[5,2,`matter`,`What happens to the particles in a solid when it is heated?`,`They vibrate faster`,[`They stop moving`,`They get smaller`,`They become a new element`],`Heating gives particles more energy; if they vibrate enough, the solid melts.`],[5,3,`matter`,`What is chromatography used for?`,`Separating the colours in an ink`,[`Measuring a temperature`,`Weighing a gas`,`Making metals harder`],`The dyes in an ink travel up wet paper at different speeds, so they separate into bands.`],[6,2,`matter`,`Which gas must be present for things to burn?`,`Oxygen`,[`Nitrogen`,`Helium`,`Argon`],`Fire needs fuel, heat and oxygen. Remove any one and the fire goes out.`],[6,2,`matter`,`What is a saturated solution?`,`One in which no more solute can dissolve`,[`One that is boiling hot`,`One with no water in it`,`One that has frozen solid`],`Stir sugar into water until crystals stay at the bottom: the solution is saturated.`],[6,3,`matter`,`Why does spreading salt on icy roads help?`,`It lowers the temperature at which water freezes`,[`It heats the road surface`,`It makes the ice heavier`,`It soaks up the cold air`],`Salty water freezes below 0 °C, so ice melts at temperatures where pure water would stay frozen.`],[6,2,`matter`,`All metals are attracted to magnets.`,`False`,[`True`],`Only a few metals, mainly iron, nickel and cobalt, are magnetic. Copper, gold and aluminium are not.`],[7,1,`matter`,`Which element has the symbol Fe?`,`Iron`,[`Fluorine`,`Fermium`,`Francium`],`Fe comes from ferrum, the Latin word for iron. Fluorine is F, fermium Fm and francium Fr.`],[7,2,`matter`,`What is an ion?`,`An atom that has gained or lost electrons`,[`An atom with extra protons only`,`A tiny piece of metal`,`A molecule of water`],`Losing electrons makes a positive ion; gaining them makes a negative ion.`],[7,2,`matter`,`Which metal conducts electricity best?`,`Silver`,[`Iron`,`Lead`,`Tin`],`Silver is the best conductor, but copper is nearly as good and far cheaper, so wires are copper.`],[7,3,`matter`,`What happens to the speed of most chemical reactions when the temperature rises?`,`They speed up`,[`They slow down`,`They stop completely`,`They stay exactly the same`],`Hotter particles move faster and collide more often and with more energy.`],[8,1,`matter`,`What is a molecule?`,`Two or more atoms joined together`,[`A single proton`,`A kind of cell`,`A charged particle in a wire`],`An oxygen molecule (O₂) has two oxygen atoms; a water molecule has three atoms.`],[8,2,`matter`,`What is the formula of carbon dioxide?`,`CO₂`,[`CO`,`C₂O`,`CH₄`],`One carbon atom bonded to two oxygen atoms. CO is carbon monoxide; CH₄ is methane.`],[8,2,`matter`,`Which of these is a hydrocarbon fuel?`,`Methane`,[`Water`,`Salt`,`Sand`],`Hydrocarbons are made only of hydrogen and carbon; methane is the main gas in natural gas.`],[8,3,`matter`,`What is electrolysis?`,`Using electricity to split a compound`,[`Making electricity in a battery`,`Measuring the charge of an atom`,`Turning a solid into a gas`],`Passing a current through water, for example, splits it into hydrogen and oxygen.`],[8,3,`matter`,`What is the relative charge of a proton?`,`+1`,[`−1`,`0`,`+2`],`Protons are positive (+1), electrons negative (−1) and neutrons have no charge.`],[8,2,`matter`,`Water boils at a lower temperature high up on a mountain.`,`True`,[`False`],`Air pressure is lower up high, so water boils below 100 °C and food takes longer to cook.`],[3,1,`energy`,`What happens to a ball you throw straight up into the air?`,`Gravity pulls it back down`,[`It keeps going up for ever`,`It stops and floats there`,`It turns sideways and flies off`],`Gravity slows the ball, stops it at the top and pulls it back to the ground.`],[3,2,`energy`,`How can you make a toy car on the floor go faster?`,`Give it a harder push`,[`Paint it a new colour`,`Make it wetter`,`Switch on its lights`],`A bigger force makes an object speed up more.`],[3,1,`energy`,`A magnet can pull a steel paperclip through a sheet of paper.`,`True`,[`False`],`Magnetic forces pass through paper, plastic and even water.`],[4,1,`energy`,`Which of these is moved along by wind energy?`,`A sailing boat`,[`A candle`,`A torch`,`A gas cooker`],`The wind pushes on the sails, which moves the boat.`],[4,3,`energy`,`What is a pulley used for?`,`Lifting loads more easily with a rope and wheel`,[`Measuring how hot something is`,`Making electricity from light`,`Cutting wood into planks`],`A pulley changes the direction of a pull, and several pulleys together make lifting easier.`],[5,1,`energy`,`Which device changes electrical energy into sound?`,`A loudspeaker`,[`A microphone`,`A solar panel`,`A battery`],`A loudspeaker's cone vibrates to make sound. A microphone does the opposite.`],[5,2,`energy`,`What is air resistance?`,`A force that slows things moving through air`,[`A force that speeds up falling objects`,`The weight of the air in a room`,`The pull of a magnet on metal`],`Air pushes against moving objects; the faster they go, the bigger this drag force.`],[5,3,`energy`,`Why does a parachute fall slowly?`,`Its large surface creates a lot of air resistance`,[`It is lighter than air`,`Gravity does not pull on cloth`,`The wind always blows upwards`],`The canopy catches a lot of air, so the drag balances the weight at a slow speed.`],[5,2,`energy`,`Heavier objects always fall faster than lighter ones.`,`False`,[`True`],`Without air resistance, all objects fall at the same rate. A feather is only slowed by the air.`],[6,1,`energy`,`Power is measured in…`,`Watts`,[`Joules`,`Newtons`,`Ohms`],`One watt is one joule of energy transferred every second.`],[6,3,`energy`,`How does a generator make electricity?`,`By turning a magnet near coils of wire`,[`By heating a battery`,`By shining light on metal`,`By mixing two chemicals`],`Moving a magnet near a coil (or a coil near a magnet) makes a current flow: electromagnetic induction.`],[7,1,`energy`,`Which meter measures electric current?`,`An ammeter`,[`A voltmeter`,`A thermometer`,`A barometer`],`An ammeter is placed in series, in the path of the current.`],[7,2,`energy`,`What does a transformer do?`,`Changes the voltage of an alternating current`,[`Turns light energy into heat energy`,`Stores electric charge for later`,`Measures the resistance of a wire`],`Power stations step voltage up for long cables; transformers near homes step it down.`],[7,3,`energy`,`Why does metal feel colder than wood at the same temperature?`,`Metal conducts heat away from your hand faster`,[`Metal is always at a lower temperature`,`Wood makes its own heat`,`Metal reflects warmth back into the room`],`Both are at room temperature, but metal carries heat from your skin away quickly, so it feels cold.`],[7,2,`energy`,`A battery stores energy in chemical form.`,`True`,[`False`],`Chemicals inside react when the circuit is complete, pushing electrons round it.`],[8,1,`energy`,`Electrical resistance is measured in…`,`Ohms`,[`Amps`,`Volts`,`Watts`],`The unit is named after Georg Ohm. Higher resistance means less current for the same voltage.`],[8,2,`energy`,`What is an object's momentum?`,`Its mass multiplied by its velocity`,[`Its weight divided by its height`,`Its speed squared`,`Its energy divided by time`],`A heavy lorry and a fast bullet can both have large momentum.`],[8,3,`energy`,`In a series circuit, what happens to the current when more bulbs are added?`,`It decreases`,[`It increases`,`It stays the same`,`It reverses direction`],`Each extra bulb adds resistance, so less current flows and the bulbs get dimmer.`],[8,3,`energy`,`Which type of radiation is stopped by a sheet of paper?`,`Alpha radiation`,[`Gamma radiation`,`X-rays`,`Radio waves`],`Alpha particles are large and slow; gamma rays can pass through thick lead.`]],[[3,1,`earth`,`Which season comes after winter?`,`Spring`,[`Summer`,`Autumn`,`The rainy season`],`The year goes round: after the cold of winter comes the warming of spring.`],[3,2,`earth`,`What do we call a long period with little or no rain?`,`A drought`,[`A flood`,`A blizzard`,`A heatwave`],`In a drought, rivers shrink and crops can fail because there is not enough water.`],[4,1,`earth`,`What do we call frozen rain that falls as balls of ice?`,`Hail`,[`Sleet`,`Fog`,`Dew`],`Hailstones grow as they are tossed up and down inside tall storm clouds.`],[4,2,`earth`,`What is a delta?`,`Land built up where a river drops mud and sand at its mouth`,[`A very deep trench in the ocean`,`A mountain made entirely of ice`,`A lake inside the crater of a volcano`],`Much of Bangladesh lies on the Ganges–Brahmaputra delta, one of the largest in the world.`],[4,3,`earth`,`What makes the wind blow?`,`Air moving from high pressure to low pressure`,[`Trees waving their branches`,`The Moon pulling on the air`,`Clouds pushing the air along`],`Uneven heating by the Sun makes differences in air pressure, and air flows between them.`],[5,1,`earth`,`What are the three main types of rock?`,`Igneous, sedimentary and metamorphic`,[`Hard, soft and medium`,`Granite, marble and chalk only`,`Lava, magma and ash`],`Rocks are grouped by how they form: from melted rock, from layers of sediment, or by heat and pressure.`],[5,2,`earth`,`What is fog?`,`A cloud that forms close to the ground`,[`Smoke from factories`,`Steam rising from the sea floor`,`Dust blown by the wind`],`When moist air near the ground cools, its water vapour condenses into tiny droplets: fog.`],[5,3,`earth`,`Why does it get colder as you climb a high mountain?`,`Air is thinner higher up and holds less heat`,[`You are closer to the cold Moon`,`Snow makes all the air cold`,`Sunlight is weaker at the top`],`The air temperature falls by roughly 6.5 °C for every kilometre you climb.`],[5,2,`earth`,`Rivers always flow from north to south.`,`False`,[`True`],`Rivers flow downhill, in whatever direction that is. The Nile, for example, flows north.`],[6,2,`earth`,`What is a fault?`,`A crack in Earth's crust where rocks can move`,[`A river that flows underground`,`A hole made by a meteorite`,`A layer of soft clay`],`Sudden movement along a fault causes an earthquake.`],[6,3,`earth`,`Where do stalactites hang from?`,`The roof of a cave`,[`The floor of a cave`,`The trees of a rainforest`,`The edges of glaciers`],`Stalactites hold tight to the ceiling; stalagmites grow up from the ground.`],[7,1,`earth`,`What are Earth's three main layers, from the outside in?`,`Crust, mantle, core`,[`Core, mantle, crust`,`Mantle, crust, core`,`Crust, core, mantle`],`The thin crust sits on the thick, hot mantle, which surrounds the metal core.`],[7,2,`earth`,`What is the main cause of acid rain?`,`Gases from burning fuels, such as sulfur dioxide`,[`Salt spray blown in from the sea`,`Pollen released by flowering plants`,`Dust storms that blow off deserts`],`These gases dissolve in rainwater and form acids that damage forests, lakes and buildings.`],[7,3,`earth`,`What is weathering?`,`Rock breaking down where it is, by weather, plants or chemicals`,[`Rock being carried away by rivers`,`Rock melting into magma underground`,`Rock forming from layers of mud`],`Weathering breaks rock in place; erosion then carries the pieces away.`],[7,1,`earth`,`Volcanoes can form new islands.`,`True`,[`False`],`Iceland and the Hawaiian Islands were built by volcanic eruptions.`],[8,1,`earth`,`What is a biome?`,`A large region with its own climate, plants and animals`,[`A single living cell in a pond`,`A type of volcanic rock`,`A layer of the upper atmosphere`],`Deserts, tropical rainforests, grasslands and tundra are biomes.`],[8,2,`earth`,`Why are places by the sea usually milder than places far inland?`,`Water heats up and cools down more slowly than land`,[`The sea is always warmer than land`,`Sand stores all of the Sun's heat`,`Waves make the air hotter`],`The sea keeps coastal summers cooler and winters warmer.`],[8,3,`earth`,`What is the Coriolis effect?`,`The turning of winds and currents caused by Earth's spin`,[`The cooling of air as it rises`,`The heating of the ground by the Sun`,`The pull of the Moon on the oceans`],`It makes storms spin anticlockwise in the northern hemisphere and clockwise in the southern.`],[3,1,`space`,`What shape is the Earth?`,`Almost a sphere (ball-shaped)`,[`Flat like a plate`,`A cube`,`A long cylinder`],`Photos from space show Earth as a ball, very slightly wider at the equator.`],[4,1,`space`,`What is the largest object in our Solar System?`,`The Sun`,[`Jupiter`,`Earth`,`Saturn`],`The Sun holds about 99.8 % of all the mass in the Solar System.`],[4,2,`space`,`About how long does the Moon take to orbit Earth?`,`About a month (27 days)`,[`About a day`,`About a week`,`About a year`],`The Moon circles Earth in about 27 days; from new moon to new moon takes about 29½ days.`],[5,1,`space`,`Which planet is known for its Great Red Spot?`,`Jupiter`,[`Mars`,`Venus`,`Neptune`],`The Great Red Spot is a giant storm on Jupiter, wider than Earth.`],[5,3,`space`,`Why do we have a leap year every four years?`,`Earth takes about 365¼ days to orbit the Sun`,[`The Moon slows down every four years`,`Clocks run fast in February`,`The Sun moves farther away each year`],`The extra quarter day adds up, so one day is added to February every fourth year.`],[5,2,`space`,`Venus spins in the opposite direction to most planets.`,`True`,[`False`],`Venus spins very slowly backwards, so on Venus the Sun rises in the west.`],[6,2,`space`,`After the Sun, which star is nearest to Earth?`,`Proxima Centauri`,[`Polaris (the North Star)`,`Sirius`,`Betelgeuse`],`Proxima Centauri is about 4.2 light-years away. Sirius is the brightest star in the night sky.`],[6,3,`space`,`Which planet spins on its side, tilted about 98 degrees?`,`Uranus`,[`Neptune`,`Saturn`,`Mars`],`Uranus may have been knocked over by a huge collision long ago.`],[7,2,`space`,`What is a meteorite?`,`A space rock that reaches the ground`,[`A shooting star that burns up completely`,`A small moon of Mars`,`A satellite built by people`],`Most meteors burn up in the air; the rare pieces that land are meteorites.`],[7,3,`space`,`Why can astronauts not breathe on the Moon without a suit?`,`It has almost no atmosphere`,[`Its air is too cold to breathe`,`Its oxygen is frozen in lakes`,`Its gravity pulls the air into the ground`],`The Moon's gravity is too weak to hold on to an atmosphere.`],[7,2,`space`,`Saturn is less dense than water.`,`True`,[`False`],`Saturn's average density is lower than water's: in a big enough bath, it would float.`],[8,1,`space`,`What is a galaxy?`,`A huge system of stars, gas and dust held together by gravity`,[`A single star much brighter than our Sun`,`A cloud of frozen water drifting in space`,`A group of planets orbiting one star`],`The Milky Way is one of perhaps two trillion galaxies in the observable universe.`],[8,2,`space`,`What is the event horizon of a black hole?`,`The boundary beyond which nothing can escape`,[`The bright ring around a planet`,`The outer edge of the Solar System`,`The point where a star is born`],`Once anything crosses the event horizon, not even light can get back out.`],[8,3,`space`,`What is a supernova?`,`The huge explosion of a dying massive star`,[`The birth of a new planet`,`A very bright comet`,`A star that has just formed`],`For a few weeks a supernova can outshine a whole galaxy.`],[4,2,`general`,`In a fair test, how many things should you change at a time?`,`Only one`,[`Two at once`,`As many as you like`,`None at all`],`Change one variable and keep everything else the same, so you know what caused any difference.`],[5,1,`general`,`What do we call the things kept the same in an experiment?`,`Control variables`,[`Results`,`Conclusions`,`Hypotheses`],`Keeping them the same makes the test fair.`],[6,1,`general`,`Which unit is used to measure mass in science?`,`The kilogram`,[`The litre`,`The metre`,`The newton`],`Mass is measured in kilograms. Weight is a force, measured in newtons.`],[6,2,`general`,`Why do scientists repeat their experiments?`,`To check that the results are reliable`,[`To make the results bigger`,`To use up the equipment`,`Because the first try never counts`],`If repeated tests give similar results, we can trust them more.`],[7,1,`general`,`What is the variable you change on purpose in an experiment called?`,`The independent variable`,[`The dependent variable`,`The control group`,`The conclusion`],`The independent variable is the one you change on purpose; the dependent variable is what you measure to see the effect.`],[7,2,`general`,`Which kind of graph best shows how something changes over time?`,`A line graph`,[`A pie chart`,`A pictogram`,`A Venn diagram`],`A line graph joins measurements in time order, so rises and falls are easy to see.`],[8,2,`general`,`Why should an experiment use a large sample?`,`Results from only a few cases may be due to chance`,[`Small samples are always wrong`,`Large samples need no repeating`,`Large samples make the test faster`],`The more cases you test, the less a few unusual ones can distort the result.`],[8,3,`general`,`What does 'theory' mean in science?`,`A well-tested explanation backed by lots of evidence`,[`A wild guess with no evidence`,`A rule that can never change`,`An experiment that failed`],`The theory of evolution and the germ theory of disease are supported by huge amounts of evidence.`]],[[3,1,`living`,`How many legs does a spider have?`,`8`,[`6`,`10`,`4`],`Spiders are arachnids, with eight legs; insects have six.`],[3,1,`living`,`Which of these animals lives in water and breathes with gills?`,`A goldfish`,[`A duck`,`An adult frog`,`A seal`],`Fish take oxygen from water with gills; seals and ducks breathe air with lungs.`],[3,2,`living`,`What do we call an animal that has no backbone, such as a worm or a snail?`,`An invertebrate`,[`A mammal`,`A reptile`,`A bird`],`Most kinds of animal are invertebrates; vertebrates have a backbone.`],[3,2,`living`,`Which group does a crab belong to?`,`Crustaceans`,[`Insects`,`Fish`,`Mammals`],`Crabs, shrimps and lobsters are crustaceans, with hard shells and jointed legs.`],[3,3,`living`,`Which part of a seed grows down into the soil first when it starts to grow?`,`The root`,[`The leaf`,`The flower`,`The fruit`],`The young root anchors the plant and starts taking in water before the shoot appears.`],[3,3,`living`,`Earthworms help to mix and loosen the soil.`,`True`,[`False`],`Their burrows let in air and water, and their droppings make the soil richer.`],[4,1,`living`,`In a food chain, what do the arrows show?`,`Which way the energy (food) passes`,[`Which animal is biggest`,`Where each animal lives`,`Which animal is fastest`],`An arrow from grass to rabbit means the rabbit eats the grass and gets its energy.`],[4,1,`living`,`Yeast, used to make bread rise, is a kind of…`,`fungus`,[`plant`,`animal`,`rock`],`Yeast is a tiny single-celled fungus that gives off carbon dioxide gas.`],[4,2,`living`,`What is an animal at the very top of a food chain, with no natural predators, called?`,`An apex predator`,[`A producer`,`A herbivore`,`A decomposer`],`Tigers in the Sundarbans and orcas in the ocean are apex predators.`],[4,2,`living`,`Why do plants grow towards a sunny window?`,`They grow towards light`,[`They are attracted to warm glass`,`They want to see outside`,`Gravity pulls them that way`],`Growing towards light (phototropism) helps leaves catch more sunlight for making food.`],[4,3,`living`,`What does ‘endangered’ mean for a species?`,`It is at risk of dying out`,[`It is dangerous to people`,`It lives only in zoos`,`It has just been discovered`],`The Royal Bengal tiger is endangered, so its home in the Sundarbans is protected to stop it dying out.`],[4,3,`living`,`Mould growing on old bread is a living thing.`,`True`,[`False`],`Mould is a fungus; it feeds on the bread and grows.`],[5,1,`living`,`Which tube in a plant carries sugar made in the leaves to other parts?`,`Phloem`,[`Xylem`,`Stomata`,`Root hairs`],`Phloem carries food; xylem carries water up from the roots.`],[5,1,`living`,`Snails, slugs and octopuses belong to which group of animals?`,`Molluscs`,[`Insects`,`Amphibians`,`Crustaceans`],`Molluscs have soft bodies, and many have a shell.`],[5,2,`living`,`Which hairs on a plant's roots soak up most of the water and minerals?`,`Root hairs`,[`Leaf hairs`,`Stem fibres`,`Petals`],`Thousands of tiny root hairs give a huge surface for taking in water.`],[5,2,`living`,`Why does a camel's hump help it survive in the desert?`,`It stores fat that can be used for energy`,[`It is full of water`,`It keeps the camel cool`,`It helps the camel see further`],`Using the fat lets a camel go a long time without food; the hump does not hold water.`],[5,3,`living`,`What is a keystone species?`,`A species that many others in its ecosystem depend on`,[`The biggest animal in a habitat`,`A species found only on islands`,`A plant that grows on stones`],`Removing a keystone species, such as sea otters or bees, can change a whole ecosystem.`],[5,3,`living`,`An invasive species is one brought to a new place where it spreads and harms local wildlife.`,`True`,[`False`],`Water hyacinth, brought to Bengal as a garden plant, now chokes many rivers and ponds.`],[6,1,`living`,`What is the word equation for photosynthesis?`,`Carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen`,[`Glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water`,`Oxygen + water → carbon dioxide + sugar`,`Sunlight + oxygen → water + soil`],`Using light energy, plants turn carbon dioxide and water into glucose, releasing oxygen.`],[6,1,`living`,`Into how many kingdoms are living things often sorted in school biology?`,`Five`,[`Two`,`Three`,`Ten`],`Animals, plants, fungi, protists and prokaryotes (bacteria) make the five-kingdom system.`],[6,2,`living`,`What does yeast produce when it respires without oxygen (ferments sugar)?`,`Alcohol and carbon dioxide`,[`Oxygen and water`,`Lactic acid only`,`Salt and water`],`The carbon dioxide makes bread dough rise, and the alcohol evaporates when it is baked.`],[6,2,`living`,`In a food chain, roughly how much of the energy in one level passes on to the next?`,`About 10 %`,[`About 90 %`,`All of it`,`About 50 %`],`Most energy is used for living or lost as heat, which is why food chains are short.`],[6,3,`living`,`What is a quadrat used for in fieldwork?`,`Counting plants or animals in a measured square`,[`Measuring rainfall in a field`,`Catching insects that fly past`,`Testing how acidic the soil is`],`Counting inside several quadrats lets scientists estimate how many live in a whole area.`],[6,3,`living`,`Insects, spiders and crabs are all arthropods, with jointed legs and hard outer skeletons.`,`True`,[`False`],`Arthropods are the largest group of animals on Earth.`],[7,1,`living`,`What builds up in your muscles when they respire without enough oxygen during hard exercise?`,`Lactic acid`,[`Carbon monoxide`,`Salt`,`Starch`],`Anaerobic respiration makes lactic acid when muscles need energy faster than oxygen can reach them.`],[7,1,`living`,`What is a recessive allele?`,`One whose effect only shows if both copies are the same`,[`One that always shows its effect`,`A gene found only in plants`,`A broken chromosome`],`A dominant allele hides a recessive one, so the recessive feature appears only with two copies.`],[7,2,`living`,`What is selective breeding?`,`Choosing parents with useful features to breed from`,[`Letting animals choose their mates`,`Changing genes in a laboratory`,`Breeding only wild animals`],`Farmers have used it for thousands of years to get cows with more milk or bigger crops.`],[7,2,`living`,`Which famous sheep, born in 1996, was the first mammal cloned from an adult cell?`,`Dolly`,[`Molly`,`Polly`,`Shaun`],`Dolly was cloned in Scotland from a cell taken from an adult sheep.`],[7,3,`living`,`What is a mutation?`,`A change in the DNA of a gene`,[`A kind of cell wall`,`A healthy diet`,`An animal's habitat`],`Most mutations have little effect, but some cause disease and a few are useful.`],[7,3,`living`,`Bacteria can become resistant to antibiotics when the medicines are overused.`,`True`,[`False`],`Surviving bacteria pass on resistance, so antibiotics should be used only when needed and the course finished.`],[8,1,`living`,`What is the difference between genotype and phenotype?`,`Genotype is the alleles; phenotype is the features that show`,[`They mean exactly the same thing`,`Genotype is height; phenotype is weight`,`Genotype is for plants; phenotype is for animals`],`Two brown-eyed people may have different genotypes, such as Bb and BB.`],[8,2,`living`,`Which process returns nitrogen from dead organisms to the soil?`,`Decomposition by bacteria and fungi`,[`Photosynthesis in leaves`,`Evaporation from rivers`,`Lightning alone`],`Decomposers break down proteins, releasing nitrogen compounds that plants can use again.`],[8,2,`living`,`Darwin's study of which birds on the Galápagos Islands helped him develop his theory of natural selection?`,`Finches`,[`Penguins`,`Parrots`,`Eagles`],`Finches on different islands had beaks suited to different foods.`],[8,3,`living`,`What is genetic engineering?`,`Changing an organism's genes directly`,[`Choosing which animals breed`,`Building machines from cells`,`Counting genes under a microscope`],`Bacteria engineered with a human gene now make insulin for people with diabetes.`],[8,3,`living`,`Most of the carbon in your body came originally from carbon dioxide in the air.`,`True`,[`False`],`Plants capture carbon dioxide in photosynthesis, and the carbon passes along food chains to us.`],[3,1,`body`,`What are the pointed teeth next to your front teeth, used for tearing food, called?`,`Canines`,[`Molars`,`Incisors`,`Wisdom teeth`],`Incisors cut, canines tear and molars grind.`],[3,2,`body`,`How many milk (baby) teeth does a child usually have?`,`20`,[`32`,`10`,`28`],`Children have 20 milk teeth, which are replaced by up to 32 adult teeth.`],[3,3,`body`,`Your heart beats even while you are asleep.`,`True`,[`False`],`It keeps pumping all the time, though it beats more slowly when you rest.`],[4,1,`body`,`Which is the longest and strongest bone in the human body?`,`The femur (thigh bone)`,[`The humerus (upper arm)`,`The tibia (shin bone)`,`The spine (backbone)`],`The femur runs from the hip to the knee.`],[4,2,`body`,`What kind of joint are your shoulder and hip, which can move in many directions?`,`Ball-and-socket joints`,[`Hinge joints`,`Fixed joints`,`Pivot joints`],`A rounded end sits in a cup, so the limb can swing and turn.`],[4,2,`body`,`Which disease is spread by the bite of an infected Aedes mosquito and is common in Bangladesh during the monsoon?`,`Dengue`,[`Cholera`,`Tuberculosis`,`Measles`],`Getting rid of standing water, where these mosquitoes breed, helps prevent dengue.`],[4,3,`body`,`Oral rehydration solution (ORS), which saves children with diarrhoea, is clean water mixed with salt and…`,`sugar`,[`lemon juice`,`milk`,`oil`],`Researchers in Dhaka (at icddr,b) helped show how well this simple mixture works.`],[4,3,`body`,`Tooth enamel is the hardest substance in the human body.`,`True`,[`False`],`Enamel covers the crown of each tooth and protects it, but acid from sugary food can damage it.`],[5,1,`body`,`The smallest bone in the human body is in the ear. What is it called?`,`The stirrup (stapes)`,[`The kneecap`,`The little toe`,`The jaw`],`The stirrup is only about 3 mm long and passes sound vibrations to the inner ear.`],[5,2,`body`,`What do tendons do?`,`Join muscles to bones`,[`Join bones to bones`,`Carry blood to the heart`,`Send messages to the brain`],`When a muscle pulls on a tendon, the bone moves; ligaments join bone to bone.`],[5,2,`body`,`Which illness is caused by a lack of vitamin C?`,`Scurvy`,[`Rickets`,`Goitre`,`Anaemia`],`Sailors once got scurvy on long voyages until they ate citrus fruit.`],[5,3,`body`,`Which disease, carried by mosquitoes, is caused by a tiny parasite called Plasmodium?`,`Malaria`,[`Dengue`,`Typhoid`,`Flu`],`The parasite lives in red blood cells and causes fever and chills.`],[5,3,`body`,`Cholera is spread mainly through dirty drinking water.`,`True`,[`False`],`Clean water and good sanitation stop cholera from spreading.`],[6,1,`body`,`Which part of the eye is a layer of light-sensitive cells at the back?`,`The retina`,[`The cornea`,`The iris`,`The lens`],`The retina turns light into nerve signals that go to the brain along the optic nerve.`],[6,2,`body`,`Which coiled part of the inner ear turns vibrations into nerve signals?`,`The cochlea`,[`The eardrum`,`The ear lobe`,`The ear canal`],`Tiny hairs inside the fluid-filled cochlea respond to different pitches.`],[6,2,`body`,`Which acid does the stomach produce to help digest food and kill germs?`,`Hydrochloric acid`,[`Citric acid`,`Sulphuric acid`,`Vinegar`],`The stomach lining makes mucus to protect itself from its own acid.`],[6,3,`body`,`What are villi in the small intestine?`,`Tiny finger-like folds that absorb food`,[`Teeth in the stomach`,`Germs that cause illness`,`Muscles that push food back up`],`Millions of villi give a huge surface area for taking digested food into the blood.`],[6,3,`body`,`A lack of iodine in the diet can cause a swelling in the neck called goitre.`,`True`,[`False`],`The thyroid gland needs iodine, which is why iodised salt is sold.`],[7,1,`body`,`When you take your pulse at the wrist, what are you counting?`,`Heartbeats, felt as surges in an artery`,[`Breaths taken each minute`,`Signals passing along nerves`,`Blood cells passing the skin`],`Each heartbeat pushes blood through the arteries, which you can feel as a pulse.`],[7,2,`body`,`What do ligaments do?`,`Hold bones together at joints`,[`Join muscles to bones`,`Make blood cells`,`Carry oxygen`],`Ligaments are strong, slightly stretchy bands; a sprain is a damaged ligament.`],[7,2,`body`,`Which organ releases insulin into the blood?`,`The pancreas`,[`The liver`,`The kidneys`,`The stomach`],`Special cells in the pancreas sense blood sugar and release insulin when it rises.`],[7,3,`body`,`What is the recovery position in first aid used for?`,`Keeping an unconscious person's airway clear`,[`Treating a broken arm or leg`,`Stopping a nosebleed quickly`,`Resting after hard exercise`],`Lying on their side lets fluid drain from the mouth so it cannot block breathing.`],[7,3,`body`,`Bones are living tissue that can repair themselves.`,`True`,[`False`],`Bone cells rebuild broken bones, which is why a cast keeps the pieces still while they heal.`],[8,1,`body`,`Why does the human circulation count as a ‘double’ circulation?`,`Blood passes through the heart twice on each full trip round the body`,[`There are two hearts`,`Blood flows in both directions in each vessel`,`Each person has two blood groups`],`One loop goes to the lungs and back; the other goes to the rest of the body.`],[8,2,`body`,`What does the body mass index (BMI) compare?`,`Weight with height`,[`Heart rate with age`,`Height with age`,`Muscle with fat directly`],`BMI = mass in kg ÷ (height in m)²; it is only a rough guide.`],[8,2,`body`,`Which hormone, made mainly in the ovaries, controls much of the menstrual cycle?`,`Oestrogen`,[`Adrenaline`,`Insulin`,`Testosterone`],`Oestrogen and progesterone work together to prepare the body for a possible pregnancy.`],[8,3,`body`,`Long-term heavy drinking of alcohol especially damages which organ?`,`The liver`,[`The lungs`,`The bladder`,`The eyes`],`The liver breaks down alcohol; too much over years can cause scarring (cirrhosis).`],[8,3,`body`,`Herd immunity means that when enough people are vaccinated, a disease struggles to spread even to those who are not.`,`True`,[`False`],`The germ runs out of people to infect, which protects babies and people who cannot have vaccines.`],[3,1,`matter`,`At what temperature does pure water freeze?`,`0 °C`,[`10 °C`,`100 °C`,`−20 °C`],`Pure water freezes at 0 °C and boils at 100 °C at sea level.`],[3,2,`matter`,`Which of these materials is magnetic?`,`Steel`,[`Wood`,`Plastic`,`Glass`],`Steel contains iron, which magnets attract.`],[3,3,`matter`,`Which material is best for making a window?`,`Glass`,[`Wood`,`Cardboard`,`Steel`],`Glass is transparent, hard and waterproof.`],[4,1,`matter`,`Which of these dissolves in water?`,`Sugar`,[`Sand`,`Pebbles`,`Wood`],`Sugar breaks up into particles too small to see and mixes evenly with the water.`],[4,2,`matter`,`What is the spreading of a smell through a room by moving particles called?`,`Diffusion`,[`Melting`,`Freezing`,`Filtering`],`Gas particles move about randomly and spread from where there are many to where there are few.`],[4,3,`matter`,`Which liquid is often called the ‘universal solvent’ because it dissolves so many things?`,`Water`,[`Oil`,`Honey`,`Petrol`],`Water dissolves more substances than any other common liquid.`],[4,3,`matter`,`Paper is made from wood.`,`True`,[`False`],`Wood is broken into fibres, mixed with water and pressed into thin sheets.`],[5,1,`matter`,`Which acid is found in lemons and oranges?`,`Citric acid`,[`Hydrochloric acid`,`Sulphuric acid`,`Nitric acid`],`Citric acid gives citrus fruit their sour taste.`],[5,1,`matter`,`What is vinegar a weak solution of?`,`Acetic acid`,[`Citric acid`,`Salt`,`Sugar`],`Vinegar is mostly water with about 5 % acetic (ethanoic) acid.`],[5,2,`matter`,`Which method separates pure water from salty water by boiling it and cooling the steam?`,`Distillation`,[`Filtering`,`Sieving`,`Magnetism`],`The water evaporates, leaving salt behind, and condenses into pure water elsewhere.`],[5,2,`matter`,`Which three things does a fire need, often shown as the fire triangle?`,`Heat, fuel and oxygen`,[`Wood, water and wind`,`Smoke, ash and air`,`Light, sound and heat`],`Remove any one of the three and the fire goes out.`],[5,3,`matter`,`Soap and toothpaste are mildly alkaline.`,`True`,[`False`],`Alkalis such as soap feel slippery and have a pH above 7.`],[6,1,`matter`,`What is often called the fourth state of matter, found in lightning and stars?`,`Plasma`,[`Steam`,`Foam`,`Gel`],`In plasma, gas is so hot that electrons are stripped from the atoms.`],[6,1,`matter`,`Which metal does the symbol Ag, from the Latin argentum, stand for?`,`Silver`,[`Gold`,`Aluminium`,`Argon`],`Argentina's name also comes from argentum, because explorers hoped to find silver there.`],[6,2,`matter`,`Which metal's symbol, Pb, comes from the Latin plumbum?`,`Lead`,[`Platinum`,`Phosphorus`,`Potassium`],`Romans used lead for water pipes, which is where the word plumbing comes from.`],[6,2,`matter`,`What colour does universal indicator turn in a strong alkali?`,`Purple`,[`Red`,`Green`,`Yellow`],`It goes red in strong acid, green when neutral and purple in strong alkali.`],[6,3,`matter`,`What are burned fuels such as methane mostly turned into?`,`Carbon dioxide and water`,[`Oxygen and hydrogen`,`Salt and water`,`Nitrogen and smoke only`],`Complete combustion of a hydrocarbon makes carbon dioxide and water, releasing energy.`],[6,3,`matter`,`Plastics are made from long chain molecules called polymers.`,`True`,[`False`],`Many small units (monomers) join to make a polymer, such as polythene.`],[7,1,`matter`,`What is the chemical formula of ammonia?`,`NH₃`,[`H₂O`,`CO₂`,`CH₄`],`One nitrogen atom is joined to three hydrogen atoms.`],[7,1,`matter`,`Which group of the periodic table contains very reactive metals, such as sodium and potassium, that fizz in water?`,`The alkali metals (Group 1)`,[`The noble gases`,`The halogens`,`The transition metals`],`They react quickly with water to make hydrogen and an alkaline solution.`],[7,2,`matter`,`Which group of elements includes chlorine, used to keep swimming pools clean?`,`The halogens`,[`The noble gases`,`The alkali metals`,`The lanthanides`],`Halogens such as fluorine and chlorine are very reactive non-metals.`],[7,2,`matter`,`Which of these metals is the least reactive?`,`Gold`,[`Potassium`,`Iron`,`Magnesium`],`Gold sits near the bottom of the reactivity series, so it is found pure and does not tarnish.`],[7,3,`matter`,`What is a composite material?`,`Two or more materials combined to get better properties`,[`A material made of one element`,`A metal mixed with salt`,`A liquid that never freezes`],`Reinforced concrete and fibreglass are composites.`],[7,3,`matter`,`A neutron has no electric charge.`,`True`,[`False`],`Protons are positive, electrons negative and neutrons neutral.`],[8,1,`matter`,`What is the mass number of an atom?`,`The number of protons plus neutrons`,[`The number of electrons only`,`The number of protons only`,`The number of shells`],`Carbon-12 has 6 protons and 6 neutrons, so its mass number is 12.`],[8,1,`matter`,`How is crude oil separated into fuels such as petrol, diesel and bitumen?`,`Fractional distillation`,[`Filtration`,`Electrolysis`,`Chromatography`],`Different parts boil at different temperatures and condense at different heights in a column.`],[8,2,`matter`,`What is graphene?`,`A single layer of carbon atoms in a honeycomb pattern`,[`A kind of soft, bendy plastic`,`A metal alloy used in coins`,`A form of glass used in screens`],`Graphene is extremely strong and conducts electricity very well.`],[8,2,`matter`,`Why are metals good conductors of electricity?`,`They have electrons that are free to move`,[`They are always cold`,`They contain water`,`Their atoms are very large`],`In metallic bonding, outer electrons form a ‘sea’ that can carry charge through the metal.`],[8,3,`matter`,`What does ‘biodegradable’ mean?`,`It can be broken down by bacteria and fungi`,[`It is made from crude oil`,`It can never be recycled`,`It is poisonous to animals`],`Biodegradable materials rot away, unlike most ordinary plastics.`],[8,3,`matter`,`In the periodic table, elements in the same period (row) have the same number of electron shells.`,`True`,[`False`],`Moving along a period adds one proton and one electron at a time to the same outer shell.`]],[[3,1,`energy`,`What can you use to see your reflection?`,`A mirror`,[`A brick`,`A sponge`,`A blanket`],`Smooth, shiny surfaces such as mirrors reflect light back in a regular way.`],[3,2,`energy`,`What is an echo?`,`A sound bouncing back off a surface`,[`A sound that never stops`,`A very quiet sound`,`A sound made by animals only`],`Shout towards a cliff and the sound reflects back to your ears.`],[3,3,`energy`,`Rubbing a balloon on your hair can make it stick to a wall because of static electricity.`,`True`,[`False`],`Rubbing moves tiny electric charges, and opposite charges attract.`],[4,1,`energy`,`Which force can act without touching, like a magnet pulling a pin?`,`A non-contact force`,[`Friction`,`Air resistance`,`A push from your hand`],`Magnetism, gravity and static electricity act across a gap.`],[4,2,`energy`,`Why does a compass needle point north?`,`The Earth acts like a giant magnet`,[`The needle is attracted to the Sun`,`Wind pushes it`,`North is colder`],`A compass needle is a small magnet that lines up with Earth's magnetic field.`],[4,2,`energy`,`Which part of a bicycle uses gears?`,`The chain and cogs that turn the back wheel`,[`The handlebars and brakes`,`The saddle and its post`,`The bell and the lights`],`Changing gear changes how far the wheel turns for each push of the pedals.`],[4,3,`energy`,`What is lightning?`,`A huge spark of static electricity`,[`Light reflected from the Moon`,`Burning gas from the ground`,`The Sun shining through rain`],`Charge builds up in storm clouds until it jumps to the ground or another cloud.`],[4,3,`energy`,`Wind power and hydroelectric power are both renewable energy resources.`,`True`,[`False`],`Wind and flowing water are replaced naturally, unlike coal or oil.`],[5,1,`energy`,`On the Moon, your weight would be about what fraction of your weight on Earth?`,`One sixth`,[`Twice as much`,`Exactly the same`,`One half`],`The Moon's gravity is about 1/6 of Earth's, but your mass stays the same.`],[5,1,`energy`,`What is the difference between mass and weight?`,`Mass is the amount of matter; weight is the pull of gravity on it`,[`They are the same thing`,`Weight is measured in kilograms; mass in newtons`,`Mass changes on the Moon but weight does not`],`Mass is in kilograms; weight is a force measured in newtons.`],[5,2,`energy`,`What kind of lens makes things look bigger, as in a magnifying glass?`,`A convex lens`,[`A flat mirror`,`A sheet of paper`,`A concave lens`],`A convex lens is thicker in the middle and bends light inwards to magnify.`],[5,2,`energy`,`What does a fuse in a plug do?`,`Melts and breaks the circuit if the current is too high`,[`Makes the appliance work faster`,`Stores spare electricity`,`Turns the appliance on and off each day`],`Breaking the circuit stops wires overheating and causing a fire.`],[5,3,`energy`,`How do bats find insects in the dark?`,`By echolocation: listening for echoes of their calls`,[`By the smell of the insects`,`By seeing heat`,`By feeling air move`],`Bats send out very high-pitched sounds and hear how they bounce back.`],[5,3,`energy`,`Loudness of sound is measured in decibels (dB).`,`True`,[`False`],`Normal talking is about 60 dB; long exposure to sounds above about 85 dB can damage hearing.`],[6,1,`energy`,`Which kind of heat transfer carries warm water up and cold water down in a heated pan?`,`Convection`,[`Conduction`,`Radiation`,`Reflection`],`Heated fluid becomes less dense and rises, setting up a convection current.`],[6,1,`energy`,`Which part of the electromagnetic spectrum do TV remote controls usually use?`,`Infrared`,[`Gamma rays`,`X-rays`,`Ultraviolet`],`An infrared LED flashes coded pulses that the TV's sensor reads.`],[6,2,`energy`,`Which waves are used to take pictures of broken bones?`,`X-rays`,[`Radio waves`,`Microwaves`,`Sound waves`],`X-rays pass through soft tissue but are absorbed by bone.`],[6,2,`energy`,`About how fast does sound travel in air?`,`About 340 metres per second`,[`About 3 metres per second`,`About 300,000 km per second`,`About 34 km per second`],`Sound is much slower than light, which is why thunder arrives after the flash.`],[6,3,`energy`,`What is the moment (turning effect) of a force equal to?`,`Force × distance from the pivot`,[`Force + distance`,`Mass ÷ time`,`Speed × time`],`This is why a long spanner makes a nut easier to turn.`],[6,3,`energy`,`Sound waves are longitudinal: the air vibrates back and forth in the direction the wave travels.`,`True`,[`False`],`Light waves are transverse; sound waves are made of squashes and stretches of air.`],[7,1,`energy`,`Which part of the electromagnetic spectrum has the longest wavelength?`,`Radio waves`,[`Gamma rays`,`Visible light`,`X-rays`],`Radio waves can be metres or even kilometres long.`],[7,1,`energy`,`Electricity bills charge for energy in which unit?`,`Kilowatt-hours (kWh)`,[`Volts (V)`,`Amperes (A)`,`Newtons (N)`],`One kilowatt-hour is the energy used by a 1 kW appliance running for one hour.`],[7,2,`energy`,`What does Hooke's law say about a spring?`,`Its stretch is proportional to the force, up to a limit`,[`It always snaps at exactly 10 N`,`It gets shorter when it is pulled`,`It stores no energy when stretched`],`Double the force and you double the stretch, until the spring is overstretched.`],[7,2,`energy`,`What is terminal velocity?`,`Top falling speed, when air resistance equals weight`,[`The speed of a train at a station`,`The speed at which light travels`,`The speed at which sound is made`],`Once the forces balance, a skydiver stops speeding up.`],[7,3,`energy`,`What is an electromagnet?`,`A coil of wire that becomes a magnet when current flows`,[`A magnet that never loses its power`,`A battery made of magnets`,`A wire that glows`],`Electromagnets can be switched on and off, for example in scrapyard cranes and door bells.`],[7,3,`energy`,`Nuclear power stations use fission, the splitting of large atoms such as uranium.`,`True`,[`False`],`Splitting uranium nuclei releases heat that boils water to drive turbines.`],[8,1,`energy`,`What is the half-life of a radioactive substance?`,`The time for half of its atoms to decay`,[`Half the time it takes to glow`,`The time it takes to become safe`,`Half the time it takes to melt`],`After two half-lives, a quarter of the original atoms remain.`],[8,1,`energy`,`What is the acceleration of a car that goes from 0 to 20 m/s in 4 seconds?`,`5 m/s²`,[`80 m/s²`,`16 m/s²`,`0.2 m/s²`],`Acceleration = change in velocity ÷ time = 20 ÷ 4 = 5 m/s².`],[8,2,`energy`,`Why does a hydraulic car jack let a small force lift a heavy car?`,`Pressure is passed through a liquid to a larger piston`,[`The liquid is lighter than air`,`Magnets inside lift the car`,`The car becomes lighter`],`The same pressure on a larger area produces a larger force.`],[8,2,`energy`,`In a motor, what makes the coil turn?`,`The force on a current-carrying wire in a magnetic field`,[`Air blowing through the coil`,`Heat flowing from the battery`,`Gravity pulling the coil down`],`This is called the motor effect.`],[8,3,`energy`,`What does a Sankey diagram show?`,`How energy is transferred, with arrow widths showing amounts`,[`The parts of a plant or animal cell`,`The layers inside the Earth`,`The order of the planets from the Sun`],`A wide arrow for wasted heat shows a machine is not very efficient.`],[8,3,`energy`,`Ultraviolet light from the Sun can cause sunburn and skin damage.`,`True`,[`False`],`UV has more energy than visible light; sunscreen absorbs or blocks much of it.`],[3,1,`earth`,`Which kind of precipitation is made of six-sided ice crystals that drift down as flakes?`,`Snow`,[`Hail`,`Dew`,`Fog`],`Snowflakes form when water vapour freezes into six-sided ice crystals in cold clouds.`],[3,2,`earth`,`What are the tiny drops of water found on grass on a cool morning called?`,`Dew`,[`Rain`,`Snow`,`Steam`],`Water vapour in the air condenses on cold surfaces overnight.`],[3,3,`earth`,`Rainbows appear when sunlight shines through raindrops.`,`True`,[`False`],`Each drop bends and splits sunlight into its colours.`],[4,1,`earth`,`Which tall, dark clouds bring thunderstorms?`,`Cumulonimbus`,[`Cirrus`,`Stratus`,`Fog`],`Cumulonimbus clouds can tower over 10 km high and bring lightning and heavy rain.`],[4,2,`earth`,`Which gas makes up about 21 % of the air?`,`Oxygen`,[`Nitrogen`,`Carbon dioxide`,`Argon`],`Air is about 78 % nitrogen, 21 % oxygen and small amounts of other gases.`],[4,3,`earth`,`What is lava?`,`Molten rock that has come out onto the surface`,[`Cold rock deep underground`,`Steam from a kettle`,`Mud at the bottom of a river`],`Underground it is called magma; once it erupts it is called lava.`],[4,3,`earth`,`Thin, wispy clouds high in the sky are called cirrus clouds.`,`True`,[`False`],`Cirrus clouds are made of ice crystals and often mean the weather will change.`],[5,1,`earth`,`What is the rock cycle?`,`The slow changing of rocks from one type into another`,[`A bicycle made of stone`,`The daily heating of rocks`,`A circle of standing stones`],`Rocks are worn down, buried, heated, melted and pushed up again over millions of years.`],[5,2,`earth`,`Why do we have seasons?`,`The Earth's axis is tilted as it orbits the Sun`,[`The Earth gets closer to and further from the Sun`,`The Sun gets hotter and colder`,`The Moon blocks the Sun in winter`],`The tilt means each hemisphere gets more direct sunlight for part of the year.`],[5,2,`earth`,`What is a mineral?`,`A natural solid with a fixed make-up and crystals`,[`Any rock found on a beach`,`A kind of plant that grows on rocks`,`Water that comes from a spring`],`Quartz, salt and diamond are minerals; rocks are made of minerals.`],[5,3,`earth`,`Which gas makes up only about 0.04 % of the air but is very important for climate?`,`Carbon dioxide`,[`Nitrogen`,`Oxygen`,`Helium`],`Though there is little of it, carbon dioxide traps heat and its amount is rising.`],[5,3,`earth`,`Soil takes hundreds of years to form, so losing it to erosion is a serious problem.`,`True`,[`False`],`A few centimetres of topsoil can take centuries to build up from weathered rock and dead plants.`],[6,1,`earth`,`On about which dates are day and night nearly equal everywhere on Earth?`,`The equinoxes, around 20 March and 22 September`,[`The solstices, in June and December`,`The first day of each month`,`Only on New Year's Day`],`At the equinoxes the Sun is overhead at the equator.`],[6,2,`earth`,`What is the longest day of the year in the Northern Hemisphere called?`,`The summer solstice`,[`The winter solstice`,`The spring equinox`,`Leap day`],`It falls around 21 June, when the North Pole is tilted most towards the Sun.`],[6,2,`earth`,`What happens in an earthquake at the epicentre?`,`It is the point on the surface right above where the quake starts`,[`It is where the quake ends`,`It is the deepest part of the ocean`,`It is where the lava comes out`],`Shaking is usually strongest near the epicentre.`],[6,3,`earth`,`What kind of volcano has gentle slopes and runny lava, like those in Hawaii?`,`A shield volcano`,[`A composite volcano`,`A dead volcano`,`A mud volcano`],`Runny lava flows a long way before cooling, building wide, gentle slopes.`],[6,3,`earth`,`Geothermal energy uses heat from inside the Earth.`,`True`,[`False`],`Iceland heats many homes with hot water from underground.`],[7,1,`earth`,`What is a geyser?`,`A hot spring that shoots water and steam into the air`,[`A cold river that flows underground`,`A volcano that has stopped erupting`,`A kind of glacier in the mountains`],`Old Faithful in Yellowstone erupts about every 90 minutes.`],[7,2,`earth`,`In freeze–thaw weathering, how does water break rocks?`,`It expands when it freezes in cracks`,[`It dissolves the rock instantly`,`It makes the rock catch fire`,`It shrinks and pulls the rock apart`],`Repeated freezing widens cracks until pieces of rock break off.`],[7,2,`earth`,`What is the main difference between a composite volcano and a shield volcano?`,`Composite volcanoes are steep, with explosive eruptions of sticky lava`,[`Composite volcanoes are always under the sea`,`Shield volcanoes never erupt`,`Shield volcanoes are made of ice`],`Mount Fuji is a composite volcano; Mauna Loa is a shield volcano.`],[7,3,`earth`,`Which layer of the atmosphere, closest to the ground, has almost all of the weather?`,`The troposphere`,[`The stratosphere`,`The thermosphere`,`The exosphere`],`The troposphere is about 8–15 km thick and contains most of the air's water vapour.`],[7,3,`earth`,`Limestone caves are carved out mainly by slightly acidic rainwater dissolving the rock.`,`True`,[`False`],`Rainwater absorbs carbon dioxide, forming weak carbonic acid that dissolves limestone.`],[8,1,`earth`,`What is a carbon sink?`,`Something that takes in more carbon than it gives out`,[`A drain that carries away dirty water`,`A deep mine where coal is dug`,`The exhaust pipe of a car`],`Protecting forests, peat bogs and mangroves keeps carbon out of the atmosphere.`],[8,2,`earth`,`Why can a tsunami wave be small in the open ocean but huge at the coast?`,`It slows and piles up as the water gets shallower`,[`It gains water from rivers`,`The wind makes it bigger`,`The Moon pulls it up at the coast`],`Its energy is squeezed into a shorter, taller wave in shallow water.`],[8,2,`earth`,`What does a seismogram from a seismometer show?`,`A record of ground shaking over time`,[`A map of the stars`,`A chart of rainfall`,`The temperature of lava`],`Scientists use seismograms from several stations to locate an earthquake.`],[8,3,`earth`,`The Earth's magnetic field protects us from much of the harmful solar wind.`,`True`,[`False`],`Charged particles are steered towards the poles, where they make the auroras.`],[3,1,`space`,`Which planet is farthest from the Sun?`,`Neptune`,[`Mars`,`Earth`,`Venus`],`Neptune is the eighth and outermost planet; Pluto is now a dwarf planet.`],[3,2,`space`,`What is a shooting star really?`,`A small space rock burning up in the air`,[`A star falling to Earth`,`An aeroplane's light`,`A firework`],`These meteors glow because they heat up rushing through the atmosphere.`],[3,3,`space`,`Stars shine in different colours, depending on how hot they are.`,`True`,[`False`],`The hottest stars look blue-white, and cooler ones look orange or red.`],[4,1,`space`,`Which planet is the smallest in our Solar System?`,`Mercury`,[`Mars`,`Neptune`,`Venus`],`Mercury is only a little bigger than our Moon.`],[4,2,`space`,`Which star stays almost still in the night sky over the North Pole and helps travellers find north?`,`Polaris (the North Star)`,[`Sirius (the Dog Star)`,`Vega (in Lyra)`,`Betelgeuse (in Orion)`],`Polaris lies almost directly above Earth's North Pole.`],[4,3,`space`,`About how old is the Sun?`,`About 4.6 billion years`,[`About 6,000 years`,`About 1 million years`,`About 100 billion years`],`The Sun and planets formed from a cloud of gas and dust about 4.6 billion years ago.`],[4,3,`space`,`An astronaut's footprints on the Moon could last for millions of years.`,`True`,[`False`],`With no wind or rain to wear them away, footprints stay until something disturbs them.`],[5,1,`space`,`Which hunter-shaped constellation has three bright stars in a row called its belt?`,`Orion`,[`The Plough`,`Leo`,`Scorpius`],`Orion is easy to spot in the winter sky.`],[5,2,`space`,`About how long does the International Space Station take to orbit the Earth once?`,`About 90 minutes`,[`About 24 hours`,`About a week`,`About 10 minutes`],`Travelling at about 28,000 km/h, the crew see around 16 sunrises a day.`],[5,2,`space`,`What are the colourful lights in the sky near the poles called?`,`Auroras`,[`Rainbows`,`Comets`,`Eclipses`],`Particles from the Sun hit the upper atmosphere and make it glow green, pink or red.`],[5,3,`space`,`What is the Kuiper Belt?`,`A ring of icy bodies beyond Neptune, including Pluto`,[`A belt worn by astronauts`,`The ring around Saturn`,`The asteroids between Mars and Jupiter`],`The Kuiper Belt holds many dwarf planets and the source of some comets.`],[5,3,`space`,`Rockets work because pushing gas backwards pushes the rocket forwards.`,`True`,[`False`],`This is Newton's third law: every action has an equal and opposite reaction.`],[6,1,`space`,`Which is the nearest large galaxy to the Milky Way?`,`The Andromeda Galaxy`,[`The Small Magellanic Cloud`,`The Sombrero Galaxy`,`The Whirlpool Galaxy`],`Andromeda is about 2.5 million light-years away and visible without a telescope on dark nights.`],[6,2,`space`,`What are the darker, cooler patches seen on the Sun's surface called?`,`Sunspots`,[`Craters`,`Comets`,`Moons`],`Sunspots are caused by strong magnetic fields and come and go in an 11-year cycle.`],[6,2,`space`,`Which spacecraft, launched in 1977, is the farthest human-made object from Earth?`,`Voyager 1`,[`Apollo 11`,`Hubble`,`Sputnik 1`],`Voyager 1 left the Sun's bubble of solar wind in 2012 and is still sending signals.`],[6,3,`space`,`About how hot is the visible surface of the Sun?`,`About 5,500 °C`,[`About 100 °C`,`About 1 million °C`,`About 500 °C`],`The core is far hotter, about 15 million °C.`],[6,3,`space`,`Saturn has more known moons than any other planet in the Solar System.`,`True`,[`False`],`Hundreds of small moons have been found around Saturn, more than for Jupiter.`],[7,1,`space`,`Which space telescope, launched in 2021, studies the universe mainly in infrared light?`,`The James Webb Space Telescope`,[`The Hubble Space Telescope`,`The Kepler telescope`,`The Arecibo telescope`],`Its large gold-coated mirror can see light from some of the first galaxies.`],[7,2,`space`,`About how long ago do scientists think the universe began, in the Big Bang?`,`About 13.8 billion years ago`,[`About 4.6 billion years ago`,`About 1 million years ago`,`About 100 years ago`],`This age comes from measuring how fast galaxies move apart and the glow left over from the Big Bang.`],[7,2,`space`,`Which country's Chandrayaan-3 mission landed near the Moon's south pole in 2023?`,`India`,[`China`,`Japan`,`Russia`],`It was the first soft landing so close to the Moon's south polar region.`],[7,3,`space`,`Why do satellites in geostationary orbit seem to stay still above one spot?`,`They orbit once a day, as fast as the Earth turns`,[`They are not moving at all`,`They are tied to the ground`,`They are very close to the ground`],`At about 36,000 km up, one orbit takes 24 hours, matching Earth's spin.`],[7,3,`space`,`Mars has the tallest known volcano in the Solar System, Olympus Mons.`,`True`,[`False`],`Olympus Mons is about 22 km high, more than twice the height of Mount Everest.`],[8,1,`space`,`What is the main gas in the atmosphere of Mars?`,`Carbon dioxide`,[`Oxygen`,`Nitrogen`,`Hydrogen`],`Mars's thin air is about 95 % carbon dioxide.`],[8,2,`space`,`What kind of star will our Sun eventually become, after it swells into a red giant?`,`A white dwarf`,[`A black hole`,`A neutron star`,`A new planet`],`Stars like the Sun are not massive enough to become black holes.`],[8,2,`space`,`What is escape velocity?`,`The speed needed to break free of a planet's gravity`,[`The top speed of a car`,`The speed of a falling apple`,`The speed at which light slows down`],`From Earth's surface it is about 11.2 km/s.`],[8,3,`space`,`What is an exoplanet?`,`A planet that orbits a star other than the Sun`,[`A planet with no moons`,`A planet made of ice`,`A former planet such as Pluto`],`Thousands of exoplanets have been found, many by spotting stars dim as planets pass in front.`],[8,3,`space`,`Light from distant galaxies is redshifted, which shows they are moving away from us.`,`True`,[`False`],`Stretching of light waves to longer (redder) wavelengths is evidence that the universe is expanding.`],[3,1,`general`,`Why should you wear safety goggles in some science experiments?`,`To protect your eyes`,[`To see in the dark`,`To look more like a scientist`,`To make the experiment faster`],`Splashes and small pieces can hurt your eyes.`],[4,1,`general`,`Which piece of equipment measures the volume of a liquid accurately?`,`A measuring cylinder`,[`A ruler`,`A thermometer`,`A magnet`],`Read the scale at the bottom of the curved surface (the meniscus) at eye level.`],[4,2,`general`,`What is the SI unit of time?`,`The second`,[`The minute`,`The hour`,`The day`],`Scientific measurements of time are based on the second.`],[5,1,`general`,`In an experiment, what is the dependent variable?`,`The one you measure`,[`The one you change on purpose`,`The one you keep the same`,`The one you forget`],`If you test how height changes a ball's bounce, the bounce is the dependent variable.`],[5,2,`general`,`What does the prefix milli- mean, as in millimetre and millilitre?`,`One thousandth`,[`One thousand`,`One hundredth`,`One million`],`1 millimetre = 1/1000 of a metre.`],[6,1,`general`,`What is an anomalous result?`,`One that does not fit the pattern of the others`,[`The best result you recorded`,`The very first result taken`,`The average of all results`],`Anomalies should be checked and repeated, not just ignored.`],[6,2,`general`,`Before a new scientific discovery is published, other experts check it. What is this called?`,`Peer review`,[`A hypothesis`,`A control`,`A census`],`Peer review helps catch mistakes before results are shared widely.`],[7,1,`general`,`Temperature in science is often measured in kelvin. What is 0 K called?`,`Absolute zero`,[`Freezing point`,`Room temperature`,`Boiling point`],`0 K (−273.15 °C) is the lowest possible temperature.`],[7,2,`general`,`What is a control group in an experiment testing a new medicine?`,`People who do not get the medicine, for comparison`,[`The doctors running the test`,`The people who get the most medicine`,`The people who chose the medicine`],`Comparing with a control group shows whether the medicine itself made the difference.`],[8,1,`general`,`What does the prefix nano- mean?`,`One billionth`,[`One thousandth`,`One millionth`,`One hundred`],`A nanometre is 0.000000001 m; atoms are a fraction of a nanometre across.`],[8,2,`general`,`What is a placebo in a medical trial?`,`A dummy treatment with no active medicine`,[`A very strong medicine`,`A kind of vaccine`,`A doctor's report`],`Comparing with a placebo shows whether a medicine works beyond people's expectations.`],[8,3,`general`,`Results are reproducible if other scientists get similar results when they repeat the experiment.`,`True`,[`False`],`Reproducible results give confidence that a finding is real and not a fluke.`]]],i=[...e(`science`,n),...r.flatMap(e=>t(`science`,e))];export{i as QUESTIONS};